window.PYQ_DATA.add({"id":"px-ncert11-bio-excretory","kind":"practice","exam":"NEET","year":null,"title":"Excretory Products and their Elimination · Practice","chapter":"Excretory Products and their Elimination","subject":"Biology","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 16 · Excretory Products and their Elimination","marking":{"mcq":[4,-1]},"rules":{},"note":"","questions":[{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-EXCRETORY-001","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Excretory Products and their Elimination","qno":1,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Excretory Products and their Elimination","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The filtrate becomes more concentrated as it flows down the descending limb of Henle's loop.\nReason (R): The descending limb is permeable to water but almost impermeable to electrolytes, and it passes through an increasingly hypertonic medullary interstitium.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. NCERT states that the filtrate is concentrated as it moves down the descending limb.\nR is true. The descending limb lets water through but almost no electrolytes, and the medullary osmolarity rises with depth.\nBecause of this, water leaves the limb by osmosis while the solutes stay inside, so the filtrate gets concentrated. R therefore correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 16 · Excretory Products and their Elimination","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-EXCRETORY-002","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Excretory Products and their Elimination","qno":2,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Excretory Products and their Elimination","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II (average values for a healthy adult):\nList-I\n(a) Blood filtered by kidneys per minute\n(b) Filtrate formed per minute\n(c) Filtrate formed per day\n(d) Urine released per day\nList-II\n(i) 125 ml\n(ii) 1.5 litres\n(iii) 1100-1200 ml\n(iv) 180 litres","image":"","options":["a-iii, b-i, c-iv, d-ii","a-i, b-iii, c-iv, d-ii","a-iii, b-i, c-ii, d-iv","a-iv, b-i, c-iii, d-ii"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(a) Blood filtered per minute is 1100-1200 ml, so a-iii.\n(b) Filtrate formed per minute (GFR) is 125 ml, so b-i.\n(c) 125 ml/min works out to about 180 L of filtrate per day, so c-iv.\n(d) Urine released is about 1.5 L per day, so d-ii.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 16 · Excretory Products and their Elimination","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-EXCRETORY-003","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Excretory Products and their Elimination","qno":3,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Excretory Products and their Elimination","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following correctly traces the path of blood through the vessels associated with a juxtamedullary nephron?","image":"","options":["Renal artery → efferent arteriole → glomerulus → afferent arteriole → vasa recta","Renal artery → afferent arteriole → peritubular capillaries → glomerulus → efferent arteriole","Renal artery → afferent arteriole → glomerulus → efferent arteriole → peritubular capillaries including vasa recta","Renal artery → vasa recta → afferent arteriole → glomerulus → efferent arteriole"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The afferent arteriole, a fine branch of the renal artery, forms the glomerulus.\nBlood leaves the glomerulus through the efferent arteriole.\nThe efferent arteriole then forms the peritubular capillaries around the tubule.\nA minute vessel of this network becomes the U-shaped vasa recta, which is prominent in juxtamedullary nephrons.\nOption (1) reverses the afferent and efferent arterioles. Options (2) and (4) place the capillary network before the glomerulus.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 16 · Excretory Products and their Elimination","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-EXCRETORY-004","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Excretory Products and their Elimination","qno":4,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Excretory Products and their Elimination","type":"mcq","question":"Which one of the following statements is incorrect?","image":"","options":["The medullary zone of kidney is divided into a few conical masses called medullary pyramids projecting into the calyces.","Inside the kidney the cortical region extends in between the medullary pyramids as renal pelvis.","Glomerulus alongwith Bowman's capsule is called the renal corpuscle.","Renal corpuscle, proximal convoluted tubule (PCT) and distal convoluted tubule (DCT) of the nephron are situated in the cortical region of kidney."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: the medulla is divided into a few conical medullary pyramids that project into the calyces.\n(2) Incorrect: the cortex extends between the medullary pyramids as renal columns (columns of Bertini). The renal pelvis is the broad funnel-shaped space inside the hilum, from which the calyces project.\n(3) Correct: the glomerulus and Bowman's capsule together form the renal corpuscle (Malpighian body).\n(4) Correct: the renal corpuscle, PCT and DCT lie in the cortex. Henle's loop dips into the medulla.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Exemplar Biology Class 11, Ch 19 (MCQ Q9)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 16 · Excretory Products and their Elimination","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-EXCRETORY-005","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Excretory Products and their Elimination","qno":5,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Excretory Products and their Elimination","type":"mcq","question":"According to NCERT, which option correctly describes the reaction of normal human urine?","image":"","options":["Slightly alkaline, pH about 7.4","Slightly acidic, pH about 6.0","Strongly acidic, pH about 3.0","Neutral, pH exactly 7.0"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT describes normal urine as a light yellow, watery fluid that is slightly acidic, with a pH of about 6.0.\nA pH of 7.4 is the slightly alkaline value of blood, not urine.\nA pH of 3.0 would be strongly acidic, and pH 7.0 is neutral. Neither matches the NCERT description.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 16 · Excretory Products and their Elimination","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-EXCRETORY-006","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Excretory Products and their Elimination","qno":6,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Excretory Products and their Elimination","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: In ammonotelic animals, the kidneys are the main organs for the removal of ammonia.\nStatement II: Some ureotelic animals retain a certain amount of urea in the kidney matrix to maintain a desired osmolarity.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Statement I is incorrect. Ammonia is removed by diffusion across body or gill surfaces as ammonium ions, and the kidneys play no significant role.\nStatement II is correct. NCERT states that some urea may be retained in the kidney matrix of some ureotelic animals to maintain osmolarity.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 16 · Excretory Products and their Elimination","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-EXCRETORY-007","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Excretory Products and their Elimination","qno":7,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Excretory Products and their Elimination","type":"mcq","question":"On an average, how much urea does a healthy adult human excrete per day?","image":"","options":["2-3 g","25-30 g","120-170 g","250-300 g"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT states that, on an average, 25-30 g of urea is excreted per day.\n2-3 g is ten times too low.\n120-170 g is the weight of one kidney, not the daily urea output.\n250-300 g is far too high.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 16 · Excretory Products and their Elimination","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-EXCRETORY-008","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Excretory Products and their Elimination","qno":8,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Excretory Products and their Elimination","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following would tend to raise blood pressure and/or GFR?\n(i) Release of renin by JG cells\n(ii) Action of angiotensin II on blood vessels\n(iii) Aldosterone-induced reabsorption of Na+ and water from distal parts of the tubule\n(iv) Release of ANF from the atria\n(v) Constrictory effect of ADH on blood vessels\n(vi) Suppression of ADH release after an increase in body fluid volume","image":"","options":["Three","Four","Five","Two"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Raises: renin starts the chain that forms angiotensin II, which raises glomerular pressure.\n(ii) Raises: angiotensin II is a powerful vasoconstrictor.\n(iii) Raises: Na+ and water retention increases blood pressure and GFR.\n(iv) Lowers: ANF causes vasodilation and acts as a check on the renin-angiotensin mechanism.\n(v) Raises: vasoconstriction by ADH increases blood pressure.\n(vi) Does not raise: this is the feedback that switches off ADH.\nSo four items, (i), (ii), (iii) and (v), raise blood pressure and/or GFR.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 16 · Excretory Products and their Elimination","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-EXCRETORY-009","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Excretory Products and their Elimination","qno":9,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Excretory Products and their Elimination","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following substances are secreted by the liver as constituents of bile, according to NCERT?\nBilirubin, waxes, cholesterol, lactic acid, drugs, vitamins","image":"","options":["2","3","5","4"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Bile contains bilirubin, biliverdin, cholesterol, degraded steroid hormones, vitamins and drugs.\nFrom the list, bilirubin is in bile.\nCholesterol is in bile.\nDrugs and vitamins are in bile.\nWaxes are eliminated in sebum, and lactic acid is eliminated in sweat, so neither is a bile constituent.\nTotal = 4.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 16 · Excretory Products and their Elimination","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-EXCRETORY-010","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Excretory Products and their Elimination","qno":10,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Excretory Products and their Elimination","type":"mcq","question":"Which one of the following is also known as antidiuretic hormone?","image":"","options":["Oxytocin","Vasopressin","Adrenaline","Calcitonin"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Key fact: antidiuretic hormone (ADH) is vasopressin. It is made by the hypothalamus and released from the neurohypophysis (posterior pituitary).\nADH increases water reabsorption from the DCT and collecting duct, so it prevents diuresis.\nOxytocin is also released from the posterior pituitary, which makes it the tempting choice. However, it acts on the uterus and mammary glands, not on water reabsorption.\nAdrenaline comes from the adrenal medulla, and calcitonin comes from the thyroid.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Exemplar Biology Class 11, Ch 19 (MCQ Q11)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 16 · Excretory Products and their Elimination","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-EXCRETORY-011","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Excretory Products and their Elimination","qno":11,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Excretory Products and their Elimination","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Analysis of urine helps in the clinical diagnosis of many metabolic disorders as well as malfunctioning of the kidney.\nStatement II: Normal urine of a healthy adult is a colourless, odourless and neutral fluid.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. Many conditions alter the characteristics of urine, so urine analysis helps diagnose metabolic disorders and kidney malfunction. Glycosuria in diabetes mellitus is one example.\nStatement II is incorrect on three counts:\n- normal urine is light yellow, not colourless;\n- it has a characteristic odour;\n- it is slightly acidic (pH about 6.0), not neutral.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 16 · Excretory Products and their Elimination","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-EXCRETORY-012","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Excretory Products and their Elimination","qno":12,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Excretory Products and their Elimination","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The Malpighian corpuscle of every nephron is located in the cortex of the kidney.\nReason (R): The loop of Henle of juxtamedullary nephrons runs deep into the medulla.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"A is true. NCERT places the Malpighian corpuscle, PCT and DCT in the cortical region for all nephrons.\nR is true. In juxtamedullary nephrons, the loop of Henle is very long and runs deep into the medulla.\nHowever, R describes the length of the loop in one type of nephron. It does not explain why the corpuscle lies in the cortex.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 16 · Excretory Products and their Elimination","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-EXCRETORY-013","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Excretory Products and their Elimination","qno":13,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Excretory Products and their Elimination","type":"mcq","question":"Antennal glands, also called green glands, perform the excretory function in which of the following animals?","image":"","options":["Cockroach","Earthworm","Prawn","Amphioxus"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Antennal (green) glands are the excretory organs of crustaceans such as prawns.\nCockroach excretes through Malpighian tubules.\nEarthworm excretes through nephridia.\nAmphioxus excretes through protonephridia (flame cells).\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 16 · Excretory Products and their Elimination","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-EXCRETORY-014","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Excretory Products and their Elimination","qno":14,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Excretory Products and their Elimination","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II:\nList-I\nA. Afferent arteriole\nB. Efferent arteriole\nC. JG cells\nD. Podocytes\nList-II\n(i) Leave filtration slits between them\n(ii) Release renin when GFR falls\n(iii) Fine branch of renal artery that forms the glomerular tuft\n(iv) Carries blood away from the glomerulus\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["A-(iv), B-(iii), C-(ii), D-(i)","A-(iii), B-(iv), C-(i), D-(ii)","A-(ii), B-(iv), C-(iii), D-(i)","A-(iii), B-(iv), C-(ii), D-(i)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"A-(iii): the afferent arteriole is a fine branch of the renal artery and forms the glomerular capillary tuft.\nB-(iv): the efferent arteriole carries blood away from the glomerulus.\nC-(ii): JG cells release renin when GFR falls.\nD-(i): podocytes leave filtration slits between them.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 16 · Excretory Products and their Elimination","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-EXCRETORY-015","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Excretory Products and their Elimination","qno":15,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Excretory Products and their Elimination","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I\nA. Afferent arteriole\nB. Efferent arteriole\nC. Vasa recta\nD. Collecting duct\nList-II\n(i) Carries blood away from the glomerulus\n(ii) Fine branch of the renal artery that forms the glomerulus\n(iii) Opens into the renal pelvis through the medullary pyramids\n(iv) U-shaped vessel running parallel to Henle's loop\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["A-(ii), B-(i), C-(iv), D-(iii)","A-(i), B-(ii), C-(iv), D-(iii)","A-(ii), B-(i), C-(iii), D-(iv)","A-(iv), B-(i), C-(ii), D-(iii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A-(ii): The afferent arteriole branches from the renal artery and forms the glomerulus.\nB-(i): The efferent arteriole carries blood away from the glomerulus.\nC-(iv): The vasa recta is a minute vessel of the peritubular network. It runs parallel to Henle's loop as a U-shaped vessel.\nD-(iii): Collecting ducts converge and open into the renal pelvis through the medullary pyramids in the calyces.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 16 · Excretory Products and their Elimination","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-EXCRETORY-016","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Excretory Products and their Elimination","qno":16,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Excretory Products and their Elimination","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: In ammonotelic animals, ammonia is generally excreted as ammonium ions by diffusion across body surfaces or through the gill surfaces in fish.\nStatement II: In ammonotelic animals, the kidneys play the major role in removing ammonia from the body.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. Ammonia is readily soluble, so it diffuses out as ammonium ions across the body surface, or across the gills in fish.\nStatement II is incorrect. NCERT states that kidneys do not play any significant role in the removal of ammonia.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 16 · Excretory Products and their Elimination","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-EXCRETORY-017","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Excretory Products and their Elimination","qno":17,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Excretory Products and their Elimination","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about glomerular filtration is correct?","image":"","options":["The blood filtered by the kidneys per minute is about half of the blood pumped by each ventricle per minute","Plasma proteins pass freely into the lumen of Bowman's capsule along with other plasma constituents","Podocytes of Bowman's capsule are arranged so as to leave minute spaces called filtration slits","The GFR of a healthy person is about 180 ml per minute"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(1) Incorrect. The kidneys filter 1100-1200 ml of blood per minute, which is about 1/5th of each ventricle's output, not half.\n(2) Incorrect. Proteins are held back during filtration, which is why the process is called ultrafiltration.\n(3) Correct. Podocytes are the epithelial cells of Bowman's capsule, and they leave filtration slits (slit pores) between them.\n(4) Incorrect. GFR is about 125 ml per minute. The figure of 180 refers to litres per day.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 16 · Excretory Products and their Elimination","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-EXCRETORY-018","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Excretory Products and their Elimination","qno":18,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Excretory Products and their Elimination","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The ANF mechanism acts as a check on the renin-angiotensin mechanism.\nReason (R): ANF causes dilation of blood vessels and thereby decreases blood pressure.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The renin-angiotensin mechanism raises blood pressure. Angiotensin II does this through vasoconstriction, and aldosterone does it by retaining Na+ and water.\nANF causes vasodilation, which lowers blood pressure. This directly opposes the renin-angiotensin effect.\nSo R is true, and it explains why A is true.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 16 · Excretory Products and their Elimination","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-EXCRETORY-019","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Excretory Products and their Elimination","qno":19,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Excretory Products and their Elimination","type":"mcq","question":"The increasing osmolarity gradient in the medullary interstitium of the human kidney is mainly caused by:","image":"","options":["Glucose and amino acids","NaCl and urea","$K^+$ and $HCO_3^-$","Uric acid and ammonia"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT states that the gradient is mainly caused by NaCl and urea.\nNaCl is moved by the ascending limb of Henle's loop. Urea is recycled between the thin ascending limb and the collecting tubule.\nGlucose and amino acids are reabsorbed in the PCT. They do not build the medullary gradient.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 16 · Excretory Products and their Elimination","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-EXCRETORY-020","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Excretory Products and their Elimination","qno":20,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Excretory Products and their Elimination","type":"mcq","question":"Suppose that during haemodialysis of a uremic patient, the dialysing fluid contained urea at the same concentration as in the patient's plasma, with all other constituents unchanged. What is the most likely outcome?","image":"","options":["Urea would be removed even faster because the membrane is porous","Plasma proteins would diffuse out into the dialysing fluid","There would be no net removal of urea from the patient's blood","Heparin would no longer be needed in the procedure"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The cellophane membrane allows molecules to move according to their concentration gradient.\nNormally urea is absent from the dialysing fluid, so it moves out of the blood.\nIf urea concentrations were equal on both sides, there would be no gradient and hence no net removal of urea. The patient's uremia would persist.\nOption (1) wrongly assumes that membrane porosity alone drives removal.\nOption (4) is wrong because heparin is still needed to prevent clotting.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 16 · Excretory Products and their Elimination","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-EXCRETORY-021","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Excretory Products and their Elimination","qno":21,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Excretory Products and their Elimination","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about the counter current mechanism are correct?\n(i) Interstitial osmolarity increases from the cortex towards the inner medulla.\n(ii) The ascending limb of Henle's loop is freely permeable to water.\n(iii) Small amounts of urea enter the thin segment of the ascending limb of Henle's loop.\n(iv) The collecting tubule transports urea back into the medullary interstitium.\n(v) Vasa recta is well developed around cortical nephrons.","image":"","options":["Two","Three","Four","Five"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: osmolarity rises from about 300 to about 1200 mOsmol$L^{-1}$.\n(ii) Incorrect: the ascending limb is impermeable to water.\n(iii) Correct: urea enters the thin ascending segment.\n(iv) Correct: the collecting tubule returns urea to the interstitium.\n(v) Incorrect: vasa recta is absent or highly reduced in cortical nephrons.\nThree statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 16 · Excretory Products and their Elimination","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-EXCRETORY-022","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Excretory Products and their Elimination","qno":22,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Excretory Products and their Elimination","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (structure) with List-II (its role in the counter current mechanism).\nList-I:\n(a) Ascending limb of Henle's loop\n(b) Descending limb of vasa recta\n(c) Ascending portion of vasa recta\n(d) Collecting tubule\nList-II:\n(i) Returns NaCl to the interstitium\n(ii) Transports urea back to the interstitium\n(iii) Transports NaCl out of the filtrate\n(iv) Receives NaCl exchanged from the ascending limb of Henle's loop","image":"","options":["(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(iii), (c)-(ii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(i), (c)-(iv), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(iii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(a)-(iii): The ascending limb of Henle's loop transports NaCl out of the filtrate.\n(b)-(iv): This NaCl is exchanged with the descending limb of vasa recta.\n(c)-(i): The ascending portion of vasa recta returns NaCl to the interstitium.\n(d)-(ii): Urea that enters the thin ascending limb is carried back to the interstitium by the collecting tubule.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 16 · Excretory Products and their Elimination","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-EXCRETORY-023","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Excretory Products and their Elimination","qno":23,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Excretory Products and their Elimination","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about the role of skin in excretion is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["Sweat is a watery fluid containing NaCl, small amounts of urea and lactic acid","The primary function of sweat is the removal of nitrogenous wastes","Sebum provides a protective oily covering for the skin","Both sweat glands and sebaceous glands can eliminate certain substances through their secretions"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: NCERT describes sweat as a watery fluid containing NaCl, small amounts of urea, lactic acid, etc.\n(2) Incorrect: the primary function of sweat is to cool the body surface. Removing wastes is only a secondary role.\n(3) Correct: sebum forms a protective oily layer on the skin.\n(4) Correct: both types of gland eliminate certain substances through their secretions.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 16 · Excretory Products and their Elimination","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-EXCRETORY-024","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Excretory Products and their Elimination","qno":24,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Excretory Products and their Elimination","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following groups of animals excretes nitrogenous wastes mainly as uric acid, in the form of a pellet or paste?","image":"","options":["Bony fishes, aquatic amphibians and aquatic insects","Mammals, terrestrial amphibians and marine fishes","Reptiles, birds, land snails and insects","Marine fishes, birds and aquatic insects"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Uricotelic animals excrete uric acid as a pellet or paste, losing very little water. NCERT names reptiles, birds, land snails and insects as uricotelic.\nOption (1) lists ammonotelic animals.\nOption (2) lists ureotelic animals.\nOption (4) mixes ureotelic marine fishes and ammonotelic aquatic insects with birds.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 16 · Excretory Products and their Elimination","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-EXCRETORY-025","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Excretory Products and their Elimination","qno":25,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Excretory Products and their Elimination","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The human kidneys are situated close to the dorsal inner wall of the abdominal cavity.\nReason (R): The kidneys lie between the levels of the first and the third lumbar vertebra.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A is true: NCERT states that the kidneys lie close to the dorsal inner wall of the abdominal cavity.\nR is false: the kidneys extend from the last thoracic vertebra to the third lumbar vertebra, not from the first lumbar vertebra.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 16 · Excretory Products and their Elimination","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-EXCRETORY-026","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Excretory Products and their Elimination","qno":26,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Excretory Products and their Elimination","type":"mcq","question":"Which hormone is released from the adrenal cortex when it is activated by angiotensin II, and which ions does it help reabsorb from the distal parts of the renal tubule?","image":"","options":["ADH; K+","Renin; H+","ANF; Cl–","Aldosterone; Na+"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Angiotensin II activates the adrenal cortex to release aldosterone.\nAldosterone causes reabsorption of Na+ and water from the distal parts of the tubule.\nThis raises blood pressure and GFR.\nADH comes from the neurohypophysis and acts mainly on water, not Na+, so option (1) is wrong.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 16 · Excretory Products and their Elimination","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-EXCRETORY-027","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Excretory Products and their Elimination","qno":27,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Excretory Products and their Elimination","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following animals is correctly matched with its excretory structure?","image":"","options":["Prawn – Malpighian tubules","Cockroach – Nephridia","Amphioxus – Protonephridia (flame cells)","Earthworm – Antennal (green) glands"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Amphioxus is a cephalochordate. It uses protonephridia, also called flame cells, like flatworms and rotifers do. So option (3) is correctly matched.\nPrawns are crustaceans and use antennal (green) glands, not Malpighian tubules.\nCockroaches, like most insects, use Malpighian tubules.\nEarthworms and other annelids use nephridia.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 16 · Excretory Products and their Elimination","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-EXCRETORY-028","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Excretory Products and their Elimination","qno":28,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Excretory Products and their Elimination","type":"mcq","question":"A marathon runner loses a large volume of body fluid through sweating. Which set of changes in kidney regulation is expected?","image":"","options":["Osmoreceptors are switched off; ADH release is suppressed; dilute urine is produced in large volume","Osmoreceptors are activated; ADH is released from the neurohypophysis; water reabsorption from the latter parts of the tubule increases and urine volume falls","ANF release increases; blood vessels dilate; urine output rises","Osmoreceptors are activated; ADH is released from the adenohypophysis; water reabsorption in Henle's ascending limb increases"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Excessive fluid loss activates the osmoreceptors, which stimulate the hypothalamus.\nADH (vasopressin) is then released from the neurohypophysis.\nADH increases water reabsorption from the latter parts of the tubule. This prevents diuresis, so less urine is made.\nOption (1) describes what happens when body fluid volume increases.\nANF is released when blood flow to the atria increases, not after fluid loss, so option (3) is wrong.\nOption (4) has two errors: ADH does not come from the adenohypophysis, and the ascending limb of Henle's loop is impermeable to water.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 16 · Excretory Products and their Elimination","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-EXCRETORY-029","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Excretory Products and their Elimination","qno":29,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Excretory Products and their Elimination","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I\n(A) Bowman's capsule\n(B) Henle's loop\n(C) Distal convoluted tubule\n(D) Efferent arteriole\nList-II\n(I) Hairpin-shaped segment with descending and ascending limbs\n(II) Forms the peritubular capillary network\n(III) Double-walled cup enclosing the glomerulus\n(IV) Opens, along with others, into a collecting duct\nChoose the correct answer:","image":"","options":["A-III, B-I, C-IV, D-II","A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II","A-I, B-III, C-II, D-IV","A-II, B-I, C-IV, D-III"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A-III: Bowman's capsule is the double-walled, cup-like start of the renal tubule that encloses the glomerulus.\nB-I: Henle's loop is hairpin-shaped, with a descending limb and an ascending limb.\nC-IV: The DCTs of many nephrons open into a straight collecting duct.\nD-II: The efferent arteriole forms the peritubular capillaries around the tubule.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 16 · Excretory Products and their Elimination","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-EXCRETORY-030","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Excretory Products and their Elimination","qno":30,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Excretory Products and their Elimination","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (excretory structure) with List-II (animal):\nList-I\n(a) Protonephridia\n(b) Nephridia\n(c) Malpighian tubules\n(d) Green glands\nList-II\n(i) Cockroach\n(ii) Prawn\n(iii) Amphioxus\n(iv) Earthworm","image":"","options":["a-iii, b-iv, c-i, d-ii","a-iv, b-iii, c-i, d-ii","a-iii, b-iv, c-ii, d-i","a-ii, b-iv, c-i, d-iii"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(a) Protonephridia: Amphioxus, a cephalochordate, has flame cells, so a-iii.\n(b) Nephridia: these occur in earthworms and other annelids, so b-iv.\n(c) Malpighian tubules: these occur in insects such as the cockroach, so c-i.\n(d) Green glands: these occur in crustaceans such as the prawn, so d-ii.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 16 · Excretory Products and their Elimination","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-EXCRETORY-031","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Excretory Products and their Elimination","qno":31,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Excretory Products and their Elimination","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following pairs is wrong?","image":"","options":["Uricotelic ---------- Birds","Ureotelic ---------- Insects","Ammonotelic ---------- Tadpole","Ureotelic ---------- Elephant"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(1) Correct pair: birds excrete uric acid, so they are uricotelic.\n(2) Wrong pair: terrestrial insects excrete uric acid, so they are uricotelic, not ureotelic.\n(3) Correct pair: tadpoles are aquatic and excrete ammonia, so they are ammonotelic.\n(4) Correct pair: elephants, like other mammals, excrete urea, so they are ureotelic.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Exemplar Biology Class 11, Ch 19 (MCQ Q8)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 16 · Excretory Products and their Elimination","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-EXCRETORY-032","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Excretory Products and their Elimination","qno":32,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Excretory Products and their Elimination","type":"mcq","question":"During urine formation, which of the following is secreted by the tubular cells into the filtrate?","image":"","options":["Glucose","Amino acids","H$^+$ ions","Plasma proteins"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"In tubular secretion, the tubular cells add substances like H$^+$, K$^+$ and ammonia to the filtrate.\nGlucose and amino acids move in the opposite direction: they are actively reabsorbed from the filtrate.\nPlasma proteins are not filtered at all, and they are not secreted into the tubule either.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 16 · Excretory Products and their Elimination","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-EXCRETORY-033","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Excretory Products and their Elimination","qno":33,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Excretory Products and their Elimination","type":"mcq","question":"Which part of the nephron allows small amounts of urea to pass into the medullary interstitium to help keep up its osmolarity?","image":"","options":["Collecting duct","Proximal convoluted tubule","Descending limb of Henle's loop","Bowman's capsule"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"NCERT states that the collecting duct allows small amounts of urea to pass into the medullary interstitium.\nThis urea, together with NaCl, maintains the high osmolarity of the inner medulla.\nThe PCT and descending limb are not described as sites that pass urea into the interstitium.\nBowman's capsule only receives the glomerular filtrate.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 16 · Excretory Products and their Elimination","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-EXCRETORY-034","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Excretory Products and their Elimination","qno":34,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Excretory Products and their Elimination","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following are mainly ureotelic?\nMammals, marine fishes, terrestrial amphibians, aquatic amphibians, reptiles, land snails, aquatic insects","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Ureotelic: mammals, marine fishes and terrestrial amphibians, which makes 3.\nAquatic amphibians and aquatic insects are ammonotelic.\nReptiles and land snails are uricotelic.\nSo exactly 3 of the listed groups are ureotelic.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 16 · Excretory Products and their Elimination","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-EXCRETORY-035","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Excretory Products and their Elimination","qno":35,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Excretory Products and their Elimination","type":"mcq","question":"According to NCERT, protonephridia are primarily concerned with:","image":"","options":["Exchange of respiratory gases","Ionic and fluid volume regulation (osmoregulation)","Excretion of uric acid as a dry pellet","Secretion of digestive enzymes"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT states that protonephridia are primarily concerned with ionic and fluid volume regulation, which is osmoregulation.\nThey are not respiratory or digestive structures.\nExcreting uric acid as pellets is a feature of uricotelic animals such as birds and reptiles. It is not the role of flame cells.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 16 · Excretory Products and their Elimination","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-EXCRETORY-036","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Excretory Products and their Elimination","qno":36,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Excretory Products and their Elimination","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following are layers through which blood is filtered during glomerular filtration?\n(i) Endothelium of glomerular blood vessels\n(ii) Basement membrane\n(iii) Epithelium of Bowman's capsule\n(iv) Brush border epithelium of PCT\n(v) Wall of the efferent arteriole","image":"","options":["Two","Three","Four","Five"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: the capillary endothelium is the first layer.\n(ii) Correct: the basement membrane lies between the endothelium and the capsule epithelium.\n(iii) Correct: the Bowman's capsule epithelium (podocytes) is the third layer.\n(iv) Incorrect: the PCT is involved in reabsorption, not filtration.\n(v) Incorrect: the efferent arteriole carries blood away from the glomerulus and plays no part in filtration.\nSo three layers.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 16 · Excretory Products and their Elimination","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-EXCRETORY-037","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Excretory Products and their Elimination","qno":37,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Excretory Products and their Elimination","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Glomerular filtration is described as ultrafiltration.\nReason (R): The filtration membrane is so fine that almost all constituents of plasma except proteins pass into the lumen of Bowman's capsule.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: NCERT calls glomerular filtration ultrafiltration.\nR is true: the three-layered membrane with filtration slits holds back plasma proteins and lets other constituents through.\nNCERT says it is \"therefore\" called ultrafiltration because of this very fine filtering. So R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 16 · Excretory Products and their Elimination","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-EXCRETORY-038","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Excretory Products and their Elimination","qno":38,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Excretory Products and their Elimination","type":"mcq","question":"Consider the following statements about the treatment of kidney failure:\n(i) In haemodialysis, blood is drained from a vein and returned to the body through an artery.\n(ii) The dialysing fluid has the same composition as plasma except for nitrogenous wastes.\n(iii) Anti-heparin is added to the cleared blood before it is returned to the body.\n(iv) The cellophane membrane allows molecules to pass according to their concentration gradient.\n(v) A donor kidney from a close relative is preferred to minimise rejection by the host's immune system.\nWhich of the statements are correct?","image":"","options":["(ii), (iii) and (iv) only","(i), (ii), (iii) and (v) only","(ii), (iii), (iv) and (v) only","(i), (ii), (iii), (iv) and (v)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Incorrect: blood is drained from an artery and returned through a vein.\n(ii) Correct: because nitrogenous wastes are absent from the fluid, they diffuse out of the blood.\n(iii) Correct: anti-heparin neutralises the heparin added earlier.\n(iv) Correct: the porous cellophane allows movement based on the concentration gradient.\n(v) Correct: a close-relative donor reduces the chance of immune rejection.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 16 · Excretory Products and their Elimination","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-EXCRETORY-039","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Excretory Products and their Elimination","qno":39,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Excretory Products and their Elimination","type":"mcq","question":"Tubular secretion is considered an important step in urine formation mainly because it helps in","image":"","options":["filtering plasma proteins out of the blood","maintaining the ionic and acid-base balance of body fluids","increasing the glomerular filtration rate","converting ammonia into urea"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Tubular cells secrete H$^+$, K$^+$ and ammonia into the filtrate.\nThis secretion helps maintain the ionic and acid-base balance of body fluids, which is why NCERT calls it an important step.\nProteins are not filtered, so option (1) is wrong.\nGFR is regulated by the JGA, not by secretion, so option (3) is wrong.\nAmmonia is converted to urea in the liver, not in the tubules, so option (4) is wrong.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 16 · Excretory Products and their Elimination","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-EXCRETORY-040","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Excretory Products and their Elimination","qno":40,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Excretory Products and their Elimination","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Malpighian tubules help in the removal of nitrogenous wastes as well as in osmoregulation.\nStatement II: Antennal glands are the excretory structures of insects such as the cockroach.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. NCERT states that Malpighian tubules remove nitrogenous wastes and also help in osmoregulation.\nStatement II is incorrect. Antennal (green) glands occur in crustaceans such as the prawn, while insects like the cockroach use Malpighian tubules.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 16 · Excretory Products and their Elimination","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-EXCRETORY-041","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Excretory Products and their Elimination","qno":41,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Excretory Products and their Elimination","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following correctly describes the permeability of the descending limb of the loop of Henle?","image":"","options":["Impermeable to water but permeable to electrolytes","Permeable to both water and electrolytes","Impermeable to both water and electrolytes","Permeable to water but almost impermeable to electrolytes"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"NCERT states that the descending limb is permeable to water but almost impermeable to electrolytes.\nWater therefore leaves the descending limb into the hyperosmotic medulla, and the filtrate becomes concentrated as it moves down.\nOption (1) describes the ascending limb, which is the reverse arrangement.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 16 · Excretory Products and their Elimination","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-EXCRETORY-042","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Excretory Products and their Elimination","qno":42,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Excretory Products and their Elimination","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II for a healthy adult human.\nList-I\n(a) Volume of urine excreted per day\n(b) pH of urine\n(c) Urea excreted per day\n(d) Colour of urine\nList-II\n(i) About 6.0\n(ii) Light yellow\n(iii) 1 to 1.5 litres\n(iv) 25-30 g","image":"","options":["a-(iii), b-(i), c-(iv), d-(ii)","a-(iv), b-(i), c-(iii), d-(ii)","a-(iii), b-(ii), c-(iv), d-(i)","a-(i), b-(iii), c-(iv), d-(ii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(a) An adult excretes 1 to 1.5 litres of urine per day, so a-(iii).\n(b) Urine is slightly acidic with a pH of about 6.0, so b-(i).\n(c) On an average, 25-30 g of urea is excreted daily, so c-(iv).\n(d) Urine is a light yellow watery fluid, so d-(ii).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 16 · Excretory Products and their Elimination","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-EXCRETORY-043","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Excretory Products and their Elimination","qno":43,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Excretory Products and their Elimination","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): During micturition, the smooth muscles of the urinary bladder contract while the urethral sphincter relaxes at the same time.\nReason (R): The CNS sends motor messages that bring about both the bladder contraction and the sphincter relaxation.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. Urine is released when the bladder's smooth muscles contract and the urethral sphincter relaxes simultaneously.\nR is true. In response to signals from the stretch receptors, the CNS passes on motor messages that start these coordinated events.\nR therefore directly explains why the two events in A occur together.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 16 · Excretory Products and their Elimination","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-EXCRETORY-044","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Excretory Products and their Elimination","qno":44,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Excretory Products and their Elimination","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: The PCT helps maintain the pH of body fluids by secreting hydrogen ions and ammonia into the filtrate and absorbing $HCO_3^-$ from it.\nStatement II: Among all segments of the nephron, reabsorption is maximum in the ascending limb of Henle's loop.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct: the PCT selectively secretes H+ and NH3 and reabsorbs $HCO_3^-$, which helps maintain pH and ionic balance.\nStatement II is incorrect: NCERT says reabsorption is minimum in the ascending limb. The ascending limb's main role is maintaining high medullary osmolarity.\nThe PCT, not the ascending limb, is the major site of reabsorption.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 16 · Excretory Products and their Elimination","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-EXCRETORY-045","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Excretory Products and their Elimination","qno":45,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Excretory Products and their Elimination","type":"mcq","question":"In the hormonal regulation of kidney function, antidiuretic hormone (ADH or vasopressin) is released into the blood from which structure?","image":"","options":["Adenohypophysis","Neurohypophysis","Adrenal cortex","Juxta glomerular apparatus"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"An excessive loss of body fluid activates osmoreceptors, which stimulate the hypothalamus.\nIn response, ADH (vasopressin) is released from the neurohypophysis, the posterior pituitary.\nThe adenohypophysis is a tempting choice because it is also part of the pituitary, but it does not release ADH.\nThe adrenal cortex releases aldosterone, and the JGA releases renin.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 16 · Excretory Products and their Elimination","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-EXCRETORY-046","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Excretory Products and their Elimination","qno":46,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Excretory Products and their Elimination","type":"mcq","question":"Filtrate in a juxta medullary nephron of a person producing concentrated urine passes the following points in order:\nP: start of the descending limb of Henle's loop\nQ: tip (hairpin bend) of the loop deep in the medulla\nR: top of the ascending limb\nS: end of the collecting duct in the inner medulla\nWhich sequence correctly shows the change in filtrate osmolarity from P to Q, Q to R and R to S?","image":"","options":["Decreases, increases, decreases","Increases, increases, remains constant","Increases, decreases, increases","Remains constant, decreases, increases"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"P to Q: water leaves the descending limb, which is permeable to water, while electrolytes stay in. Osmolarity increases.\nQ to R: electrolytes leave the ascending limb, which is impermeable to water. The filtrate is diluted, so osmolarity decreases.\nR to S: the collecting duct runs down through the hypertonic medulla, and water passes out readily. The urine becomes concentrated (up to about 1200 mOsmol$L^{-1}$), so osmolarity increases.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 16 · Excretory Products and their Elimination","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-EXCRETORY-047","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Excretory Products and their Elimination","qno":47,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Excretory Products and their Elimination","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following are located in the cortical region of the human kidney?\nMalpighian corpuscle, Proximal convoluted tubule, Distal convoluted tubule, Columns of Bertini, Medullary pyramids, Loop of Henle of juxtamedullary nephrons","image":"","options":["3","4","5","2"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"In the cortex:\n- The Malpighian corpuscle, the PCT and the DCT all lie in the cortical region.\n- The Columns of Bertini are extensions of the cortex that run between the medullary pyramids.\n\nNot in the cortex:\n- Medullary pyramids are conical masses of the medulla.\n- In juxtamedullary nephrons, the loop of Henle runs deep into the medulla.\n\nSo 4 of the listed structures are cortical.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 16 · Excretory Products and their Elimination","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-EXCRETORY-048","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Excretory Products and their Elimination","qno":48,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Excretory Products and their Elimination","type":"mcq","question":"A person's glomerular blood pressure falls sharply. Which of the following statements about the body's regulatory response is incorrect?","image":"","options":["JG cells release renin, which converts angiotensinogen to angiotensin I and then to angiotensin II","Angiotensin II stimulates the adrenal cortex to release aldosterone","Atrial Natriuretic Factor causes vasoconstriction and thereby reinforces the renin-angiotensin mechanism","Aldosterone promotes reabsorption of Na+ and water from the distal parts of the tubule"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(1) Correct. A fall in glomerular pressure or GFR triggers JG cells to release renin. Renin converts angiotensinogen to angiotensin I, which is then converted to angiotensin II.\n(2) Correct. Angiotensin II activates the adrenal cortex to secrete aldosterone.\n(3) Incorrect. ANF is released when blood flow to the atria increases. It causes vasodilation and lowers blood pressure, so it acts as a check on the renin-angiotensin mechanism.\n(4) Correct. Aldosterone increases Na+ and water reabsorption in the distal tubule, which raises blood pressure and GFR.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 16 · Excretory Products and their Elimination","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-EXCRETORY-049","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Excretory Products and their Elimination","qno":49,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Excretory Products and their Elimination","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Terrestrial adaptation required animals to produce urea and uric acid instead of ammonia.\nReason (R): Urea and uric acid are less toxic than ammonia, so they can be excreted with less loss of water.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. Life on land needed less toxic wastes so that water could be conserved.\nR is true. Urea and uric acid are less toxic than ammonia. Uric acid in particular is removed with minimum water loss.\nR explains A: on land water is scarce, so wastes that need little water for removal were favoured.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 16 · Excretory Products and their Elimination","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-EXCRETORY-050","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Excretory Products and their Elimination","qno":50,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Excretory Products and their Elimination","type":"mcq","question":"In which segment of the nephron are nearly all the essential nutrients and 70-80 per cent of the electrolytes and water reabsorbed?","image":"","options":["Distal convoluted tubule","Ascending limb of Henle's loop","Proximal convoluted tubule","Collecting duct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"NCERT identifies the PCT as the major site of reabsorption.\nNearly all essential nutrients, such as glucose and amino acids, are reabsorbed here.\nThe PCT also reabsorbs 70-80% of the electrolytes and water.\nThe DCT and collecting duct reabsorb smaller amounts that are adjusted to the body's needs.\nReabsorption is minimum in the ascending limb.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 16 · Excretory Products and their Elimination","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-EXCRETORY-051","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Excretory Products and their Elimination","qno":51,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Excretory Products and their Elimination","type":"mcq","question":"A minute vessel of the peritubular capillary network runs parallel to Henle's loop and forms a 'U' shape. This vessel is called:","image":"","options":["Vasa recta","Efferent arteriole","Collecting duct","Column of Bertini"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The efferent arteriole leaving the glomerulus forms the peritubular capillaries around the renal tubule.\nA minute vessel of this network runs parallel to Henle's loop and forms the U-shaped vasa recta.\nThe collecting duct is a tubule, not a blood vessel.\nThe Columns of Bertini are extensions of the cortex between the medullary pyramids.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 16 · Excretory Products and their Elimination","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-EXCRETORY-052","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Excretory Products and their Elimination","qno":52,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Excretory Products and their Elimination","type":"mcq","question":"According to NCERT, the osmolarity of the interstitial fluid in the inner medulla of the human kidney is about:","image":"","options":["300 mOsmol$L^{-1}$","600 mOsmol$L^{-1}$","1200 mOsmol$L^{-1}$","2400 mOsmol$L^{-1}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The counter current mechanism keeps up an osmolarity gradient that rises from the cortex towards the inner medulla.\nIt is about 300 mOsmol$L^{-1}$ in the cortex and about 1200 mOsmol$L^{-1}$ in the inner medulla.\n300 mOsmol$L^{-1}$ is the cortical value, so it is the most tempting wrong option.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 16 · Excretory Products and their Elimination","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-EXCRETORY-053","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Excretory Products and their Elimination","qno":53,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Excretory Products and their Elimination","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following substances present in the glomerular filtrate is reabsorbed by passive transport?","image":"","options":["Glucose","Amino acids","Na$^+$","Nitrogenous wastes"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"NCERT states that glucose, amino acids and Na$^+$ are reabsorbed actively by the tubular epithelial cells.\nNitrogenous wastes, such as urea, are reabsorbed by passive transport.\nNa$^+$ is a tempting choice because it is an ion, but NCERT lists it as actively reabsorbed.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 16 · Excretory Products and their Elimination","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-EXCRETORY-054","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Excretory Products and their Elimination","qno":54,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Excretory Products and their Elimination","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following are reabsorbed from the glomerular filtrate by active mechanisms?\nGlucose, amino acids, Na$^+$, urea, water (in the initial segments of the nephron)","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Glucose is reabsorbed actively.\nAmino acids are reabsorbed actively.\nNa$^+$ is reabsorbed actively.\nUrea is a nitrogenous waste, so it is reabsorbed passively.\nWater in the initial segments of the nephron is reabsorbed passively.\nSo 3 of the five are reabsorbed actively.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 16 · Excretory Products and their Elimination","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-EXCRETORY-055","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Excretory Products and their Elimination","qno":55,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Excretory Products and their Elimination","type":"mcq","question":"Sebaceous glands of the skin eliminate which of the following through sebum?","image":"","options":["NaCl, urea and lactic acid","Bilirubin and biliverdin","Sterols, hydrocarbons and waxes","Ammonium ions and uric acid"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Sebum from sebaceous glands carries sterols, hydrocarbons and waxes. It also forms a protective oily covering on the skin.\nNaCl, urea and lactic acid are components of sweat, which comes from the sweat glands. This makes option (1) the most tempting mistake.\nBilirubin and biliverdin are bile substances secreted by the liver.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 16 · Excretory Products and their Elimination","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-EXCRETORY-056","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Excretory Products and their Elimination","qno":56,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Excretory Products and their Elimination","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about glomerular filtration are correct?\n(i) The kidneys filter about 1100-1200 ml of blood per minute.\n(ii) This volume is roughly one-fifth of the blood pumped out by each ventricle per minute.\n(iii) Glomerular capillary blood pressure is the force that drives filtration.\n(iv) The GFR of a healthy person is about 125 litres per day.\n(v) The JGA is formed by cellular modifications of the PCT and the efferent arteriole where they meet.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: 1100-1200 ml of blood is filtered per minute.\n(ii) Correct: this is about 1/5th of the output of each ventricle per minute.\n(iii) Correct: glomerular capillary blood pressure causes filtration.\n(iv) Incorrect: GFR is about 125 ml/min, which equals about 180 L/day.\n(v) Incorrect: the JGA forms where the DCT contacts the afferent arteriole.\nSo three statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 16 · Excretory Products and their Elimination","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-EXCRETORY-057","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Excretory Products and their Elimination","qno":57,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Excretory Products and their Elimination","type":"mcq","question":"Match the terms given in Column I with their physiological processes given in Column II and choose the correct answer\nColumn I — Column II\nA. Proximal convoluted tubule — i. Formation of concentrated urine\nB. Distal convoluted tubule — ii. Filtration of blood\nC. Henle's loop — iii. Reabsorption of 70-80% of electrolytes\nD. Counter-current mechanism — iv. Ionic balance\nE. Renal corpuscle — v. maintenance of concentration gradient in medulla","image":"","options":["A-iii, B-v, C-iii, D-ii, E-i","A-iii, B-iv, C-i, D-v, E-ii","A-i, B-iii, C-ii, D-v, E-iv","A-iii, B-i, C-iv, D-v, E-ii"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"A. PCT reabsorbs about 70-80% of electrolytes and water → iii.\nB. DCT does conditional reabsorption of Na⁺ and water and secretes H⁺ and K⁺, so it maintains ionic balance → iv.\nC. Henle's loop helps form concentrated urine → i.\nD. The counter-current mechanism maintains the concentration gradient in the medulla → v.\nE. The renal corpuscle carries out filtration of blood → ii.\nThis gives A-iii, B-iv, C-i, D-v, E-ii.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Exemplar Biology Class 11, Ch 19 (MCQ Q12)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 16 · Excretory Products and their Elimination","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-EXCRETORY-058","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Excretory Products and their Elimination","qno":58,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Excretory Products and their Elimination","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Release of urine from the urinary bladder is initiated as the bladder fills with urine.\nReason (R): Stretch receptors in the wall of the filling bladder send signals to the CNS. The CNS then sends motor messages that contract the bladder's smooth muscles and relax the urethral sphincter.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. The signal for micturition is initiated by the stretching of the bladder as it fills with urine.\nR is true. Stretch receptors signal the CNS, and the CNS's motor messages cause the bladder muscles to contract while the urethral sphincter relaxes.\nR describes the very reflex (the micturition reflex) that produces A, so R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 16 · Excretory Products and their Elimination","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-EXCRETORY-059","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Excretory Products and their Elimination","qno":59,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Excretory Products and their Elimination","type":"mcq","question":"Which one of the following statements about excretory structures in animals is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["Most invertebrates have simple tubular excretory structures, whereas vertebrates have complex tubular kidneys","Nephridia of earthworms remove nitrogenous wastes and also maintain fluid and ionic balance","Flame cells are the excretory structures of all annelids","Green glands perform excretion in crustaceans"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: NCERT contrasts simple tubular structures in most invertebrates with complex kidneys in vertebrates.\n(2) Correct: nephridia remove nitrogenous wastes and maintain fluid and ionic balance.\n(3) Incorrect: only some annelids have protonephridia. Earthworms and other annelids have nephridia.\n(4) Correct: antennal (green) glands are the excretory organs of crustaceans.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 16 · Excretory Products and their Elimination","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-EXCRETORY-060","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Excretory Products and their Elimination","qno":60,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Excretory Products and their Elimination","type":"mcq","question":"In an adult human, the kidneys lie close to the dorsal inner wall of the abdominal cavity. Between which vertebral levels are they situated?","image":"","options":["Between the last cervical and the third thoracic vertebra","Between the last thoracic and the third lumbar vertebra","Between the first lumbar and the fifth lumbar vertebra","Between the third lumbar and the first sacral vertebra"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT describes the kidneys as reddish brown, bean-shaped organs close to the dorsal inner wall of the abdominal cavity.\nThey lie between the levels of the last thoracic and the third lumbar vertebra.\nOption (3) is tempting because it names only lumbar vertebrae. However, the kidneys begin higher, at the level of the last thoracic vertebra.\nOptions (1) and (4) place the kidneys in the chest or the pelvis, which is wrong.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 16 · Excretory Products and their Elimination","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-EXCRETORY-061","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Excretory Products and their Elimination","qno":61,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Excretory Products and their Elimination","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following pairs of excretory structure and animal is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["Protonephridia – Amphioxus","Nephridia – Earthworm","Malpighian tubules – Cockroach","Green glands – Planaria"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: protonephridia (flame cells) occur in the cephalochordate Amphioxus, as well as in flatworms and rotifers. \n(2) Correct: nephridia are the excretory structures of earthworms and other annelids. \n(3) Correct: Malpighian tubules are found in most insects, including cockroaches. \n(4) Incorrect: green glands (antennal glands) are found in crustaceans such as prawns. Planaria has protonephridia. \nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 16 · Excretory Products and their Elimination","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-EXCRETORY-062","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Excretory Products and their Elimination","qno":62,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Excretory Products and their Elimination","type":"mcq","question":"A patient's kidney biopsy shows inflammation of the glomeruli. The condition is called:","image":"","options":["Renal calculi","Glomerulonephritis","Uremia","Glycosuria"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT defines glomerulonephritis as inflammation of the glomeruli of the kidney.\nRenal calculi are stones, or insoluble masses of crystallised salts such as oxalates, formed in the kidney.\nUremia is the accumulation of urea in blood caused by kidney malfunction.\nGlycosuria is the presence of glucose in urine, which indicates diabetes mellitus.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 16 · Excretory Products and their Elimination","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-EXCRETORY-063","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Excretory Products and their Elimination","qno":63,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Excretory Products and their Elimination","type":"mcq","question":"The proximal convoluted tubule (PCT) has a large surface area for reabsorption. Which epithelium lines it?","image":"","options":["Simple squamous epithelium","Simple cuboidal brush border epithelium","Stratified cuboidal epithelium","Ciliated columnar epithelium"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT states that the PCT is lined by simple cuboidal brush border epithelium.\nThe brush border (microvilli) increases the surface area available for reabsorption.\nSimple squamous epithelium is thin and suited to diffusion and filtration, not bulk reabsorption.\nStratified and ciliated epithelia do not line the PCT.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 16 · Excretory Products and their Elimination","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-EXCRETORY-064","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Excretory Products and their Elimination","qno":64,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Excretory Products and their Elimination","type":"mcq","question":"Which one of the following statements about nitrogenous excretion is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["In ureotelic animals, urea formed in the liver is carried by blood to the kidneys, where it is filtered and excreted","Uricotelic animals such as reptiles and birds excrete uric acid as a pellet or paste with minimum water loss","Marine fishes are ammonotelic because they live surrounded by plenty of water","Ammonia can be eliminated by diffusion because it is readily soluble in water"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: the liver forms urea, which is released into the blood and then filtered and excreted by the kidneys.\n(2) Correct: reptiles and birds remove uric acid as a pellet or paste with minimum water loss.\n(3) Incorrect: NCERT groups marine fishes with ureotelic animals. Many bony fishes are ammonotelic, but marine fishes mainly excrete urea.\n(4) Correct: ammonia is readily soluble, so it diffuses out as ammonium ions.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 16 · Excretory Products and their Elimination","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-EXCRETORY-065","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Excretory Products and their Elimination","qno":65,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Excretory Products and their Elimination","type":"mcq","question":"In a diagram of a nephron placed within a kidney section, which part of the nephron is shown dipping into the medulla, while the other parts stay in the cortex?","image":"","options":["Malpighian corpuscle","Proximal convoluted tubule","Loop of Henle","Distal convoluted tubule"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The Malpighian corpuscle, PCT and DCT of a nephron all lie in the cortical region of the kidney. \nOnly the loop of Henle dips into the medulla. It runs only a little way in cortical nephrons and deep into the medulla in juxtamedullary nephrons. \nThe PCT is a tempting choice because it is long and coiled, but it is cortical. \nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 16 · Excretory Products and their Elimination","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-EXCRETORY-066","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Excretory Products and their Elimination","qno":66,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Excretory Products and their Elimination","type":"mcq","question":"A frog larva (tadpole) lives in water, while the adult frog lives mostly on land. Based on the modes of nitrogenous excretion described for amphibians, which change is expected during this transition?","image":"","options":["Shift from uricotelism to ammonotelism, with uric acid released by diffusion through the skin","Shift from ammonotelism to ureotelism, with the liver converting ammonia into urea","Shift from ureotelism to uricotelism, with uric acid excreted as a paste","No change, because all amphibians are ammonotelic throughout life"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Aquatic amphibians are ammonotelic. Water is plentiful, so they can lose toxic ammonia by diffusion.\nTerrestrial amphibians are ureotelic, because water must be conserved on land.\nIn ureotelic animals, the liver converts ammonia into the less toxic urea, which the kidneys then excrete.\nSo a water-dwelling larva becoming a land-dwelling adult shifts from ammonotelism to ureotelism.\nUricotelism belongs to reptiles, birds, land snails and insects, not amphibians.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 16 · Excretory Products and their Elimination","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-EXCRETORY-067","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Excretory Products and their Elimination","qno":67,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Excretory Products and their Elimination","type":"mcq","question":"The excretory structures of a cockroach are:","image":"","options":["Malpighian tubules","Nephridia","Antennal glands","Protonephridia"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"NCERT states that Malpighian tubules are the excretory structures of most insects, including cockroaches.\nNephridia belong to annelids, antennal glands to crustaceans, and protonephridia to flatworms, rotifers and similar animals.\nAn arthropod link might make antennal glands look tempting, but these occur in crustaceans, not insects.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 16 · Excretory Products and their Elimination","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-EXCRETORY-068","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Excretory Products and their Elimination","qno":68,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Excretory Products and their Elimination","type":"mcq","question":"If each ventricle of a person's heart pumps 5.5 litres of blood per minute, about how much blood would the kidneys filter per minute? Use the NCERT proportion.","image":"","options":["About 550 ml","About 1100 ml","About 2750 ml","About 125 ml"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: blood pumped by each ventricle = 5.5 L/min.\nNCERT fact: the kidneys filter roughly 1/5th of the blood pumped by each ventricle per minute.\nCalculation: 5500 ml × 1/5 = 1100 ml per minute. This matches the NCERT range of 1100-1200 ml.\nThe value 125 ml is the GFR, which is filtrate formed per minute, not blood filtered.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 16 · Excretory Products and their Elimination","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-EXCRETORY-069","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Excretory Products and their Elimination","qno":69,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Excretory Products and their Elimination","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (nephron segment) with List-II (feature):\nList-I\n(A) Proximal convoluted tubule\n(B) Descending limb of Henle's loop\n(C) Ascending limb of Henle's loop\n(D) Distal convoluted tubule\nList-II\n(I) Impermeable to water; electrolytes move out actively or passively\n(II) Conditional reabsorption of Na+ and water\n(III) Reabsorbs 70-80% of electrolytes and water\n(IV) Permeable to water, almost impermeable to electrolytes","image":"","options":["A-III, B-I, C-IV, D-II","A-II, B-IV, C-I, D-III","A-III, B-IV, C-II, D-I","A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"(A) PCT: it is the major site of reabsorption, taking back 70-80% of electrolytes and water, so A matches III.\n(B) Descending limb: permeable to water only, so B matches IV.\n(C) Ascending limb: impermeable to water, with electrolytes transported out, so C matches I.\n(D) DCT: conditional reabsorption of Na+ and water occurs here, so D matches II.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 16 · Excretory Products and their Elimination","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-EXCRETORY-070","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Excretory Products and their Elimination","qno":70,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Excretory Products and their Elimination","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following enter the kidney through the hilum?","image":"","options":["Ureter, blood vessels and nerves","Urethra, blood vessels and nerves","Ureter and collecting ducts only","Renal columns and blood vessels"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The hilum is a notch towards the centre of the inner concave surface of the kidney.\nThe ureter, blood vessels and nerves enter the kidney through it.\nThe urethra arises from the urinary bladder, not the kidney.\nCollecting ducts and renal columns are internal structures of the kidney.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 16 · Excretory Products and their Elimination","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-EXCRETORY-071","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Excretory Products and their Elimination","qno":71,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Excretory Products and their Elimination","type":"mcq","question":"Which one of the following statements is incorrect?","image":"","options":["Birds and land snails are uricotelic animals.","Mammals and frogs are ureotelic animals","Aquatic amphibians and aquatic insects are ammonotelic animals","Birds and reptiles are ureotelic"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: birds and land snails excrete uric acid, so they are uricotelic.\n(2) Correct: mammals and adult frogs excrete urea, so they are ureotelic.\n(3) Correct: aquatic amphibians and aquatic insects excrete ammonia, so they are ammonotelic.\n(4) Incorrect: birds and reptiles are uricotelic, not ureotelic.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Exemplar Biology Class 11, Ch 19 (MCQ Q7)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 16 · Excretory Products and their Elimination","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-EXCRETORY-072","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Excretory Products and their Elimination","qno":72,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Excretory Products and their Elimination","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The vasa recta is absent or highly reduced in cortical nephrons.\nReason (R): In cortical nephrons, the loop of Henle is very long and runs deep into the medulla.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A is true. NCERT states that the vasa recta is absent or highly reduced in cortical nephrons.\nR is false. In cortical nephrons (the majority of nephrons), the loop of Henle is short and extends only a little into the medulla.\nLong loops that run deep into the medulla belong to juxtamedullary nephrons.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 16 · Excretory Products and their Elimination","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-EXCRETORY-073","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Excretory Products and their Elimination","qno":73,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Excretory Products and their Elimination","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: In the majority of nephrons of the human kidney, the loop of Henle is long and runs deep into the medulla.\nStatement II: The vasa recta is a minute vessel of the peritubular capillary network, which is formed by the efferent arteriole.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Statement I is incorrect. In the majority of nephrons, the loop of Henle is short and extends only a little into the medulla. These are cortical nephrons. Long loops are found only in some nephrons, the juxtamedullary ones.\nStatement II is correct. The efferent arteriole forms the peritubular capillaries around the tubule, and a minute vessel of this network forms the U-shaped vasa recta.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 16 · Excretory Products and their Elimination","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-EXCRETORY-074","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Excretory Products and their Elimination","qno":74,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Excretory Products and their Elimination","type":"mcq","question":"In a healthy individual, the glomerular filtration rate (GFR) is approximately:","image":"","options":["1200 ml/minute","125 ml/minute","180 ml/minute","1.5 ml/minute"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"GFR is the amount of filtrate formed by the kidneys per minute.\nIn a healthy person it is about 125 ml/minute, which is about 180 L per day.\nThe value 1100-1200 ml/minute is the volume of blood filtered per minute, not the filtrate formed. That makes option (1) the tempting trap.\nThe value 180 belongs to litres per day, not ml per minute.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 16 · Excretory Products and their Elimination","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-EXCRETORY-075","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Excretory Products and their Elimination","qno":75,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Excretory Products and their Elimination","type":"mcq","question":"A person loses a large volume of blood in an accident, causing a fall in blood volume, blood pressure and GFR. How many of the following responses are expected?\n(i) Increased release of renin from JG cells\n(ii) Increased formation of angiotensin II\n(iii) Increased release of aldosterone from the adrenal cortex\n(iv) Increased release of ADH from the neurohypophysis\n(v) Increased release of ANF from the atria","image":"","options":["Two","Three","Four","Five"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Expected. A fall in glomerular blood pressure or GFR activates the JG cells to release renin.\n(ii) Expected. Renin leads to angiotensin I and then angiotensin II.\n(iii) Expected. Angiotensin II stimulates the adrenal cortex to release aldosterone.\n(iv) Expected. Excessive fluid loss activates osmoreceptors, so the hypothalamus causes ADH release.\n(v) Not expected. ANF is released when blood flow to the atria increases. Here atrial blood flow falls, and ANF would oppose the restoration of blood pressure.\nSo four responses are expected.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 16 · Excretory Products and their Elimination","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-EXCRETORY-076","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Excretory Products and their Elimination","qno":76,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Excretory Products and their Elimination","type":"mcq","question":"A healthy adult has a GFR of 125 mL per minute and passes 1.5 litres of urine in a day. Approximately what percentage of the glomerular filtrate is reabsorbed by the renal tubules?","image":"","options":["80%","90%","95%","99%"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Given: GFR = 125 mL/min; urine output = 1.5 L/day. \nFiltrate per day = 125 mL × 1440 min = 180,000 mL = 180 L. \nVolume reabsorbed = 180 − 1.5 = 178.5 L. \nPercentage reabsorbed = (178.5/180) × 100 ≈ 99%. \nThis matches the NCERT statement that nearly 99 per cent of the filtrate is reabsorbed. \nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 16 · Excretory Products and their Elimination","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-EXCRETORY-077","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Excretory Products and their Elimination","qno":77,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Excretory Products and their Elimination","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (substance) with List-II (how the nephron handles it):\nList-I\n(a) Glucose\n(b) Urea\n(c) K$^+$\n(d) Plasma proteins\nList-II\n(i) Secreted into the filtrate by tubular cells\n(ii) Not filtered; retained in the blood\n(iii) Reabsorbed by active transport\n(iv) Reabsorbed by passive transport","image":"","options":["a-iii, b-iv, c-i, d-ii","a-iv, b-iii, c-i, d-ii","a-iii, b-iv, c-ii, d-i","a-i, b-iv, c-iii, d-ii"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(a) Glucose is reabsorbed actively, so a matches (iii).\n(b) Urea is a nitrogenous waste and is reabsorbed passively, so b matches (iv).\n(c) K$^+$ is secreted into the filtrate by tubular cells, so c matches (i).\n(d) Plasma proteins cannot pass the filtration membranes in ultrafiltration, so they stay in the blood and d matches (ii).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 16 · Excretory Products and their Elimination","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-EXCRETORY-078","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Excretory Products and their Elimination","qno":78,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Excretory Products and their Elimination","type":"mcq","question":"Suppose the tubular cells of a person's nephrons lose the ability to secrete H$^+$ ions into the filtrate, while filtration and reabsorption stay normal. Which change is most likely to follow?","image":"","options":["Glucose will start appearing in the urine","H$^+$ will accumulate in body fluids, tending to lower their pH","Body fluids will become more alkaline because less H$^+$ is lost","The GFR will rise sharply to compensate"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Secreting H$^+$ into the filtrate is one way the tubules remove acid and keep the acid-base balance of body fluids.\nIf this secretion stops, H$^+$ is not removed and builds up in body fluids, so their pH tends to fall.\nOption (3) reverses this: H$^+$ that is retained makes body fluids more acidic, not alkaline.\nGlucose reabsorption is unaffected, so option (1) is wrong.\nGFR is not raised by H$^+$ secretion failure, so option (4) is wrong.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 16 · Excretory Products and their Elimination","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-EXCRETORY-079","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Excretory Products and their Elimination","qno":79,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Excretory Products and their Elimination","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Conditional reabsorption of $Na^+$ and water takes place in the distal convoluted tubule.\nStatement II: Reabsorption in the nephron is maximum in the ascending limb of Henle's loop.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct: NCERT describes conditional reabsorption of $Na^+$ and water in the DCT.\nStatement II is incorrect: reabsorption is minimum in the ascending limb.\nThe ascending limb's main role is maintaining the high osmolarity of the medullary interstitial fluid.\nMaximum reabsorption occurs in the PCT.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 16 · Excretory Products and their Elimination","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-EXCRETORY-080","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Excretory Products and their Elimination","qno":80,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Excretory Products and their Elimination","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): During haemodialysis, nitrogenous wastes move out of the blood into the dialysing fluid.\nReason (R): The dialysing fluid has the same composition as plasma except that it lacks nitrogenous wastes.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. The porous cellophane membrane lets nitrogenous wastes pass from the blood into the surrounding fluid.\nR is true. NCERT states the dialysing fluid matches plasma in composition except for nitrogenous wastes.\nR explains A. Because the fluid has no nitrogenous wastes, a concentration gradient exists, and these wastes diffuse out of the blood. Other plasma components stay balanced because their concentrations are equal on both sides.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 16 · Excretory Products and their Elimination","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-EXCRETORY-081","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Excretory Products and their Elimination","qno":81,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Excretory Products and their Elimination","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following animals is uricotelic?","image":"","options":["Pigeon","Rohu (a bony fish)","Human","Aquatic insect larva"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Reptiles, birds, land snails and insects are uricotelic. They excrete uric acid as a pellet or paste.\nA pigeon is a bird, so it is uricotelic.\nMany bony fishes and aquatic insects are ammonotelic.\nHumans are mammals, and mammals are ureotelic.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 16 · Excretory Products and their Elimination","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-EXCRETORY-082","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Excretory Products and their Elimination","qno":82,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Excretory Products and their Elimination","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following are mainly ureotelic?\nMammals, marine fishes, many terrestrial amphibians, reptiles, aquatic amphibians, land snails, birds","image":"","options":["Two","Three","Four","Five"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Ureotelic: mammals, marine fishes and many terrestrial amphibians. That makes 3.\nUricotelic: reptiles, birds and land snails.\nAmmonotelic: aquatic amphibians.\nSo three of the listed groups are ureotelic.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 16 · Excretory Products and their Elimination","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-EXCRETORY-083","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Excretory Products and their Elimination","qno":83,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Excretory Products and their Elimination","type":"mcq","question":"In a healthy adult, about 180 litres of glomerular filtrate is formed per day, but only about 1.5 litres of urine is released. What does this comparison indicate?","image":"","options":["About 1 per cent of the filtrate is reabsorbed by the renal tubules","About 50 per cent of the filtrate is reabsorbed by the renal tubules","Nearly 99 per cent of the filtrate is reabsorbed by the renal tubules","Most of the filtrate is lost through sweat and lungs"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Filtrate formed per day is about 180 L, and urine released is only about 1.5 L.\nSo almost all of the filtrate must return to the blood. NCERT puts this at nearly 99 per cent, and the process is called reabsorption.\nOption (1) wrongly treats the 1 per cent that leaves as urine as the reabsorbed part.\nLungs and skin do not account for the missing volume; the renal tubules reabsorb it.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 16 · Excretory Products and their Elimination","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-EXCRETORY-084","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Excretory Products and their Elimination","qno":84,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Excretory Products and their Elimination","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: As the filtrate moves up the ascending limb of Henle's loop, it becomes more concentrated because water leaves it.\nStatement II: The vasa recta is absent or highly reduced in cortical nephrons.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is incorrect.\n- The ascending limb is impermeable to water, so water cannot leave the filtrate here.\n- Electrolytes pass out into the medullary fluid, so the filtrate actually becomes diluted as it rises.\n- The filtrate gets concentrated in the descending limb, where water leaves.\n\nStatement II is correct. NCERT states that the vasa recta is absent or highly reduced in cortical nephrons.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 16 · Excretory Products and their Elimination","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-EXCRETORY-085","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Excretory Products and their Elimination","qno":85,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Excretory Products and their Elimination","type":"mcq","question":"Human kidneys can produce urine that is concentrated nearly how many times compared with the initial filtrate?","image":"","options":["Two times","Four times","Ten times","Hundred times"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The initial filtrate is about 300 mOsmol$L^{-1}$, roughly the same as plasma.\nThe urine can reach about 1200 mOsmol$L^{-1}$, which matches the inner medullary interstitium.\n1200/300 = 4, so human urine can be nearly four times as concentrated as the filtrate.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 16 · Excretory Products and their Elimination","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-EXCRETORY-086","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Excretory Products and their Elimination","qno":86,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Excretory Products and their Elimination","type":"mcq","question":"Sebum, the secretion of the sebaceous glands, helps to eliminate which of the following groups of substances?","image":"","options":["NaCl, urea and lactic acid","Sterols, hydrocarbons and waxes","Bilirubin, biliverdin and cholesterol","Urea, H$^+$ and K$^+$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Sebaceous glands remove sterols, hydrocarbons and waxes through sebum, which forms a protective oily layer on the skin. \nNaCl, urea and lactic acid are found in sweat, which comes from the sweat glands. This makes option (1) the most tempting wrong choice. \nBilirubin, biliverdin and cholesterol are bile substances secreted by the liver. \nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 16 · Excretory Products and their Elimination","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-EXCRETORY-087","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Excretory Products and their Elimination","qno":87,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Excretory Products and their Elimination","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (nephron segment) with List-II (function).\nList-I\n(a) PCT\n(b) Descending limb of Henle's loop\n(c) DCT\n(d) Collecting duct\nList-II\n(i) Conditional reabsorption of $Na^+$ and water\n(ii) Reabsorption of nearly all essential nutrients\n(iii) Large amounts of water reabsorbed to produce concentrated urine\n(iv) Concentration of the filtrate through water loss only","image":"","options":["a-ii, b-iii, c-i, d-iv","a-i, b-iv, c-ii, d-iii","a-ii, b-iv, c-i, d-iii","a-iii, b-iv, c-i, d-ii"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(a) PCT reabsorbs nearly all essential nutrients, so a-ii.\n(b) The descending limb is permeable to water only, so it concentrates the filtrate: b-iv.\n(c) The DCT carries out conditional reabsorption of $Na^+$ and water: c-i.\n(d) The collecting duct reabsorbs large amounts of water to produce concentrated urine: d-iii.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 16 · Excretory Products and their Elimination","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-EXCRETORY-088","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Excretory Products and their Elimination","qno":88,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Excretory Products and their Elimination","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about the counter current mechanism in the human kidney are correct?\n(i) Filtrate flows in the same direction in both limbs of Henle's loop.\n(ii) Small amounts of urea enter the thin segment of the ascending limb of Henle's loop.\n(iii) Interstitial osmolarity decreases from the cortex towards the inner medulla.\n(iv) Blood flows through the two limbs of vasa recta in a counter current pattern.\n(v) The interstitial gradient allows easy passage of water out of the collecting tubule.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Incorrect: the filtrate flows in opposite directions in the two limbs, which is what makes it a counter current.\n(ii) Correct: urea enters the thin ascending limb and is later returned to the interstitium by the collecting tubule.\n(iii) Incorrect: osmolarity increases from about 300 to about 1200 $mOsmol\\,L^{-1}$ towards the inner medulla.\n(iv) Correct: the two limbs of vasa recta also carry blood in a counter current pattern.\n(v) Correct: the gradient draws water out of the collecting tubule, concentrating urine about four times.\nThree statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 16 · Excretory Products and their Elimination","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-EXCRETORY-089","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Excretory Products and their Elimination","qno":89,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Excretory Products and their Elimination","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Vasa recta is absent or highly reduced in cortical nephrons.\nStatement II: In juxtamedullary nephrons, the loop of Henle is very short and extends only a little into the medulla.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. The U-shaped vasa recta runs parallel to Henle's loop, and NCERT states that it is absent or highly reduced in cortical nephrons. \nStatement II is incorrect. A short loop that barely enters the medulla describes cortical nephrons, which are the majority. \nJuxtamedullary nephrons have very long loops that run deep into the medulla. \nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 16 · Excretory Products and their Elimination","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-EXCRETORY-090","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Excretory Products and their Elimination","qno":90,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Excretory Products and their Elimination","type":"mcq","question":"During glomerular filtration, blood is filtered through three layers before the filtrate reaches the lumen of Bowman's capsule. Which of the following is NOT one of these three layers?","image":"","options":["Endothelium of glomerular blood vessels","Basement membrane between the two cell layers","Epithelium of Bowman's capsule","Brush border epithelium of the proximal convoluted tubule"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"NCERT lists three filtration layers: the endothelium of the glomerular capillaries, the epithelium of Bowman's capsule, and a basement membrane between them.\nOptions (1), (2) and (3) are these three layers.\nThe brush border cuboidal epithelium lines the PCT. The PCT is a later part of the tubule that reabsorbs substances, so it plays no part in filtration.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 16 · Excretory Products and their Elimination","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-EXCRETORY-091","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Excretory Products and their Elimination","qno":91,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Excretory Products and their Elimination","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: In the majority of human nephrons, the loop of Henle is short and extends only very little into the medulla.\nStatement II: The vasa recta is well developed in cortical nephrons.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. Most nephrons have a short loop of Henle that barely enters the medulla, and these are called cortical nephrons.\nStatement II is incorrect. NCERT states that the vasa recta is absent or highly reduced in cortical nephrons. It is well developed in juxtamedullary nephrons, whose long loops run deep into the medulla.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 16 · Excretory Products and their Elimination","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-EXCRETORY-092","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Excretory Products and their Elimination","qno":92,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Excretory Products and their Elimination","type":"mcq","question":"The osmolarity gradient in the medullary interstitium of the human kidney is mainly caused by which pair of substances?","image":"","options":["Glucose and amino acids","NaCl and urea","$K^+$ and $H^+$ ions","Urea and uric acid"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT states that the medullary gradient is caused mainly by NaCl and urea.\nNaCl is moved by the ascending limb of Henle's loop and the vasa recta. Urea is recycled through the thin ascending limb and the collecting tubule.\nGlucose and amino acids are almost fully reabsorbed in the PCT, so they do not build the gradient.\n$K^+$ and $H^+$ are secreted to keep ionic and pH balance.\nUric acid is not the main human nitrogenous waste.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 16 · Excretory Products and their Elimination","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-EXCRETORY-093","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Excretory Products and their Elimination","qno":93,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Excretory Products and their Elimination","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about the human kidney are correct?\n(i) Kidneys are reddish brown, bean-shaped organs.\n(ii) The hilum is present on the outer convex surface of the kidney.\n(iii) Each kidney has nearly one million nephrons.\n(iv) Calyces are projections of the renal pelvis.\n(v) The medulla forms the outer zone inside the kidney.","image":"","options":["Two","Four","Three","Five"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: NCERT describes the kidneys as reddish brown and bean shaped.\n(ii) Incorrect: the hilum lies on the inner concave surface.\n(iii) Correct: each kidney has nearly one million nephrons.\n(iv) Correct: the renal pelvis has projections called calyces.\n(v) Incorrect: the cortex is the outer zone and the medulla is the inner zone.\nSo three statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 16 · Excretory Products and their Elimination","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-EXCRETORY-094","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Excretory Products and their Elimination","qno":94,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Excretory Products and their Elimination","type":"mcq","question":"Which segment of the nephron is permeable to water but almost impermeable to electrolytes, so that the filtrate becomes concentrated as it moves through it?","image":"","options":["Ascending limb of Henle's loop","Distal convoluted tubule","Descending limb of Henle's loop","Proximal convoluted tubule"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The descending limb of Henle's loop lets water leave into the hypertonic medullary interstitium. It does not let electrolytes pass.\nBecause water leaves while solutes stay, the filtrate becomes more concentrated as it moves down.\nThe ascending limb has the opposite property: it is impermeable to water but transports electrolytes, so option (1) is wrong.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 16 · Excretory Products and their Elimination","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-EXCRETORY-095","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Excretory Products and their Elimination","qno":95,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Excretory Products and their Elimination","type":"mcq","question":"Atrial Natriuretic Factor (ANF) is released when there is:","image":"","options":["a fall in glomerular blood pressure, and it causes vasoconstriction","an increase in blood flow to the atria of the heart, and it causes vasodilation","an excessive loss of body fluid, and it causes water reabsorption","a fall in GFR, and it stimulates aldosterone release"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"ANF is released when blood flow to the atria of the heart increases.\nIt causes vasodilation, which lowers blood pressure.\nIn this way it acts as a check on the renin-angiotensin mechanism.\nOption (1) is wrong because a fall in glomerular pressure triggers renin release, not ANF, and ANF dilates rather than constricts vessels.\nOption (3) describes the stimulus and action of ADH, and option (4) describes the JGA pathway.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 16 · Excretory Products and their Elimination","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-EXCRETORY-096","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Excretory Products and their Elimination","qno":96,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Excretory Products and their Elimination","type":"mcq","question":"Which set correctly gives the approximate size (length × width × thickness) and weight of an adult human kidney?","image":"","options":["5-7 cm × 2-3 cm × 1-2 cm; 120-170 g","10-12 cm × 5-7 cm × 2-3 cm; 250-300 g","15-18 cm × 8-10 cm × 4-5 cm; 120-170 g","10-12 cm × 5-7 cm × 2-3 cm; 120-170 g"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"NCERT gives an adult kidney as 10-12 cm long, 5-7 cm wide and 2-3 cm thick.\nIts average weight is 120-170 g.\nOption (2) has the correct dimensions but an inflated weight.\nOptions (1) and (3) have the correct weight but wrong dimensions.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 16 · Excretory Products and their Elimination","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-EXCRETORY-097","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Excretory Products and their Elimination","qno":97,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Excretory Products and their Elimination","type":"mcq","question":"Atrial Natriuretic Factor (ANF) is released when blood flow to the atria of the heart increases. What is its effect on blood vessels and blood pressure?","image":"","options":["Vasoconstriction; blood pressure rises","Vasodilation; blood pressure falls","Vasoconstriction; blood pressure falls","Vasodilation; blood pressure rises"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"ANF causes vasodilation, which means the blood vessels widen.\nWider vessels lower the blood pressure.\nThis is the opposite of angiotensin II and ADH, both of which constrict blood vessels and raise blood pressure.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 16 · Excretory Products and their Elimination","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-EXCRETORY-098","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Excretory Products and their Elimination","qno":98,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Excretory Products and their Elimination","type":"mcq","question":"When excessive fluid is lost from the body, the hypothalamus is stimulated to release antidiuretic hormone (ADH). From which structure is ADH released into the blood?","image":"","options":["Adenohypophysis","Adrenal cortex","Neurohypophysis","Juxta glomerular cells"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Osmoreceptors sense the fluid loss and stimulate the hypothalamus.\nThe hypothalamus then causes ADH (vasopressin) to be released from the neurohypophysis, the posterior pituitary.\nThe adenohypophysis is the tempting wrong choice, but it does not release ADH.\nThe adrenal cortex releases aldosterone, and JG cells release renin.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 16 · Excretory Products and their Elimination","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-EXCRETORY-099","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Excretory Products and their Elimination","qno":99,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Excretory Products and their Elimination","type":"mcq","question":"Arrange the following events of micturition in the correct sequence:\n(a) Stretch receptors on the bladder wall send signals to the CNS\n(b) The urinary bladder fills with urine and gets stretched\n(c) The CNS passes on motor messages\n(d) The bladder smooth muscles contract and the urethral sphincter relaxes\n(e) Urine is released through the urethra","image":"","options":["b → a → c → d → e","a → b → c → d → e","b → c → a → d → e","b → a → d → c → e"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Step 1 (b): filling with urine stretches the bladder.\nStep 2 (a): the stretch receptors on the bladder wall send signals to the CNS.\nStep 3 (c): the CNS responds with motor messages.\nStep 4 (d): the bladder smooth muscles contract and the urethral sphincter relaxes.\nStep 5 (e): urine is released through the urethra.\nOption (4) is wrong because the muscles cannot act before the CNS sends motor messages.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 16 · Excretory Products and their Elimination","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-EXCRETORY-100","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Excretory Products and their Elimination","qno":100,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Excretory Products and their Elimination","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Reabsorption of water in the initial segments of the nephron occurs passively.\nStatement II: Glucose present in the filtrate is reabsorbed by passive transport.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. NCERT states that water is reabsorbed passively in the initial segments of the nephron.\nStatement II is incorrect. Glucose is reabsorbed actively, just like amino acids and Na$^+$.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 16 · Excretory Products and their Elimination","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-EXCRETORY-101","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Excretory Products and their Elimination","qno":101,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Excretory Products and their Elimination","type":"mcq","question":"A healthy adult has a glomerular filtration rate of 125 ml per minute and passes 1.5 litres of urine in a day. Approximately what percentage of the glomerular filtrate formed in a day is reabsorbed by the renal tubules?","image":"","options":["0.83%","99.17%","99.91%","91.7%"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: GFR = 125 ml/min; urine output = 1.5 L/day.\nFormula: daily filtrate = GFR × 1440 min; % reabsorbed = (filtrate − urine)/filtrate × 100.\nSubstituting: filtrate = 125 × 1440 = 180000 ml = 180 L; reabsorbed = (180 − 1.5)/180 × 100.\nSo about 99.17% is reabsorbed, matching NCERT's 'nearly 99 per cent'.\nWhy not (1): 0.83% is the fraction excreted, not reabsorbed.\nWhy not (3): 99.91% comes from wrongly using the 1200 ml/min of blood instead of the filtrate.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 16 · Excretory Products and their Elimination","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-EXCRETORY-102","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Excretory Products and their Elimination","qno":102,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Excretory Products and their Elimination","type":"mcq","question":"Which one of the following statements about the counter current mechanism in the human kidney is correct?","image":"","options":["NaCl from the ascending limb of Henle's loop is returned to the interstitium by the descending limb of the vasa recta","The osmolarity of the medullary interstitium rises from about 1200 mOsmol$L^{-1}$ near the cortex to about 300 mOsmol$L^{-1}$ in the inner medulla","Small amounts of urea enter the thin segment of the ascending limb of Henle's loop and are transported back to the interstitium by the collecting tubule","The filtrate becomes progressively more dilute as it moves down the descending limb of Henle's loop"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(1) Wrong. NaCl from the ascending limb of Henle's loop is exchanged with the descending vasa recta, but it is returned to the interstitium by the ascending vasa recta.\n(2) Wrong. The values are reversed: osmolarity rises from 300 mOsmol$L^{-1}$ in the cortex to about 1200 mOsmol$L^{-1}$ in the inner medulla.\n(3) Correct. This is the urea recycling step described in NCERT.\n(4) Wrong. Water leaves the descending limb, so the filtrate becomes more concentrated as it moves down.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 16 · Excretory Products and their Elimination","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-EXCRETORY-103","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Excretory Products and their Elimination","qno":103,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Excretory Products and their Elimination","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about the functions of nephron segments is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["The PCT is lined by simple cuboidal brush border epithelium, which increases the surface area for reabsorption","The descending limb of Henle's loop is impermeable to water but permeable to electrolytes","Conditional reabsorption of Na$^+$ and water takes place in the DCT","The collecting duct allows small amounts of urea to pass into the medullary interstitium"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(1) Correct. The brush border of the PCT increases the surface area for reabsorption.\n(2) Incorrect. It is the reverse: the descending limb is permeable to water but almost impermeable to electrolytes. This is why the filtrate becomes concentrated as it moves down.\n(3) Correct. Na$^+$ and water reabsorption in the DCT is conditional.\n(4) Correct. Urea passing out of the collecting duct helps keep up the medullary osmolarity.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 16 · Excretory Products and their Elimination","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-EXCRETORY-104","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Excretory Products and their Elimination","qno":104,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Excretory Products and their Elimination","type":"mcq","question":"A person suffers heavy blood loss, causing a fall in glomerular blood pressure and GFR. Which sequence correctly shows how the kidney mechanism restores GFR?","image":"","options":["Atria release ANF → vasoconstriction → rise in glomerular blood pressure → GFR restored","JG cells release renin → angiotensinogen converted to angiotensin I and then angiotensin II → vasoconstriction and aldosterone release from adrenal cortex → $Na^+$ and water reabsorption from distal tubule → rise in blood pressure and GFR","JG cells release angiotensinogen → renin converts it to aldosterone → aldosterone causes vasodilation → rise in GFR","Adrenal medulla releases aldosterone → aldosterone stimulates JG cells to release renin → renin directly constricts afferent arteriole → rise in GFR"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"A fall in glomerular blood pressure or GFR activates the JG cells, which release renin.\nRenin converts angiotensinogen into angiotensin I, which is then converted into angiotensin II.\nAngiotensin II constricts blood vessels and stimulates the adrenal cortex (not medulla) to release aldosterone.\nAldosterone increases $Na^+$ and water reabsorption from the distal tubule, raising blood pressure and GFR.\nANF causes vasodilation and opposes this mechanism, so option (1) is wrong.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 16 · Excretory Products and their Elimination","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-EXCRETORY-105","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Excretory Products and their Elimination","qno":105,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Excretory Products and their Elimination","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about micturition are correct?\n(i) Urine is stored in the urinary bladder until a voluntary signal is given by the CNS.\n(ii) The stretch receptors that start the micturition signal are located in the walls of the ureters.\n(iii) The neural mechanism that causes the release of urine is called the micturition reflex.\n(iv) The urethral sphincter contracts at the moment urine is released.\n(v) The signal for micturition is initiated by stretching of the bladder as it fills.","image":"","options":["Two","Three","Four","Five"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: urine is stored in the bladder until the CNS gives a voluntary signal.\n(ii) Incorrect: the stretch receptors are on the walls of the bladder, not the ureters.\n(iii) Correct: the neural mechanism is the micturition reflex.\n(iv) Incorrect: the sphincter relaxes during urine release.\n(v) Correct: filling stretches the bladder, which starts the signal.\nThat makes three correct statements.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 16 · Excretory Products and their Elimination","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-EXCRETORY-106","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Excretory Products and their Elimination","qno":106,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Excretory Products and their Elimination","type":"mcq","question":"In ureotelic animals, ammonia produced by metabolism is converted into urea in which organ?","image":"","options":["Kidney","Liver","Lungs","Urinary bladder"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"In ureotelic animals, ammonia formed during metabolism is converted into urea in the liver.\nThe urea is released into the blood. The kidneys then filter it and excrete it.\nThe kidney removes urea but does not make it, so option (1) is wrong.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 16 · Excretory Products and their Elimination","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-EXCRETORY-107","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Excretory Products and their Elimination","qno":107,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Excretory Products and their Elimination","type":"mcq","question":"Approximately how many nephrons are present in each human kidney?","image":"","options":["About ten thousand","About one lakh","About one million","About one hundred million"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"NCERT states that each kidney has nearly one million complex tubular structures called nephrons. These are the functional units of the kidney.\nOne lakh (100,000) is ten times too few.\nOne hundred million is far too many.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 16 · Excretory Products and their Elimination","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-EXCRETORY-108","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Excretory Products and their Elimination","qno":108,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Excretory Products and their Elimination","type":"mcq","question":"In an adult human, the kidneys lie between which vertebral levels?","image":"","options":["Between the last cervical and the first thoracic vertebra","Between the last thoracic and the third lumbar vertebra","Between the third lumbar and the first sacral vertebra","Between the first and the fifth thoracic vertebra"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT places the kidneys between the levels of the last thoracic and the third lumbar vertebra.\nThey lie close to the dorsal inner wall of the abdominal cavity.\nThe other ranges are cervical/thoracic, lower lumbar–sacral or upper thoracic levels, so none of them matches.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 16 · Excretory Products and their Elimination","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-EXCRETORY-109","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Excretory Products and their Elimination","qno":109,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Excretory Products and their Elimination","type":"mcq","question":"Which nitrogenous waste is the most toxic and needs a large amount of water for its elimination?","image":"","options":["Uric acid","Urea","Ammonia","Creatinine"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Animals excrete three major nitrogenous wastes: ammonia, urea and uric acid.\nAmmonia is the most toxic of these, so it must be diluted in a large amount of water before it is eliminated.\nUric acid is the least toxic and can be removed with very little water loss.\nUrea is less toxic than ammonia, so it is a tempting choice, but it is not the most toxic.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 16 · Excretory Products and their Elimination","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-EXCRETORY-110","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Excretory Products and their Elimination","qno":110,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Excretory Products and their Elimination","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following plasma constituents is almost completely absent from the glomerular filtrate of a healthy person?","image":"","options":["Glucose","Urea","Proteins","Sodium ions"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Blood is filtered so finely that almost all plasma constituents pass into Bowman's capsule, except the proteins.\nGlucose, urea and Na+ are all present in the filtrate.\nGlucose and Na+ are reabsorbed later in the tubule, but they are first filtered.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 16 · Excretory Products and their Elimination","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-EXCRETORY-111","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Excretory Products and their Elimination","qno":111,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Excretory Products and their Elimination","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II:\nList-I\n(A) ADH\n(B) Angiotensin II\n(C) Aldosterone\n(D) ANF\nList-II\n(i) Causes vasodilation and lowers blood pressure\n(ii) Powerful vasoconstrictor that also stimulates the adrenal cortex\n(iii) Facilitates water reabsorption from latter parts of the tubule, preventing diuresis\n(iv) Causes reabsorption of $Na^+$ and water from distal parts of the tubule","image":"","options":["A-(iv), B-(ii), C-(iii), D-(i)","A-(iii), B-(i), C-(iv), D-(ii)","A-(ii), B-(iii), C-(iv), D-(i)","A-(iii), B-(ii), C-(iv), D-(i)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"(A) ADH (vasopressin) increases water reabsorption in the later tubule parts and prevents diuresis, so A-(iii).\n(B) Angiotensin II constricts blood vessels and makes the adrenal cortex release aldosterone, so B-(ii).\n(C) Aldosterone causes reabsorption of $Na^+$ and water from the distal tubule, so C-(iv).\n(D) ANF dilates blood vessels and lowers blood pressure, acting as a check on the renin-angiotensin mechanism, so D-(i).\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 16 · Excretory Products and their Elimination","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-EXCRETORY-112","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Excretory Products and their Elimination","qno":112,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Excretory Products and their Elimination","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): As the filtrate moves up the ascending limb of Henle's loop, it becomes progressively diluted.\nReason (R): The ascending limb is impermeable to water but allows transport of electrolytes, actively or passively, into the medullary fluid.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: NCERT says the concentrated filtrate gets diluted as it passes upward in the ascending limb.\nR is true: the ascending limb is impermeable to water but transports electrolytes.\nR explains A. Electrolytes leave the tubule while water cannot follow, so the solute concentration of the filtrate falls.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 16 · Excretory Products and their Elimination","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-EXCRETORY-113","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Excretory Products and their Elimination","qno":113,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Excretory Products and their Elimination","type":"mcq","question":"A fall in GFR triggers the renin-angiotensin mechanism. Which sequence of events is correct?","image":"","options":["JG cells release renin → angiotensinogen converted to angiotensin I → angiotensin II formed → adrenal cortex releases aldosterone","Adrenal cortex releases renin → angiotensin II formed → angiotensin I formed → aldosterone released from JG cells","JG cells release aldosterone → renin converts angiotensin II to angiotensinogen → vasodilation","JG cells release renin → angiotensin I converted directly to angiotensinogen → ANF released from adrenal cortex"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Step 1: A fall in GFR activates the JG cells, which release renin.\nStep 2: Renin converts angiotensinogen in the blood to angiotensin I, which is then converted to angiotensin II.\nStep 3: Angiotensin II stimulates the adrenal cortex to release aldosterone.\nOption (2) wrongly places renin in the adrenal cortex and reverses angiotensin I and II.\nOptions (3) and (4) misplace aldosterone and ANF.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 16 · Excretory Products and their Elimination","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-EXCRETORY-114","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Excretory Products and their Elimination","qno":114,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Excretory Products and their Elimination","type":"mcq","question":"During haemodialysis, which substance is added to the blood drained from an artery before it is pumped into the dialysing unit?","image":"","options":["Heparin","Anti-heparin","Thrombin","Fibrinogen"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Blood drained from a convenient artery is pumped into the dialysing unit after an anticoagulant is added.\nNCERT names this anticoagulant as heparin. It prevents the blood from clotting inside the artificial kidney.\nAnti-heparin is the tempting wrong option. It is added only when the cleared blood is returned to the body through a vein.\nThrombin and fibrinogen promote clotting, so they would defeat the purpose.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 16 · Excretory Products and their Elimination","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-EXCRETORY-115","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Excretory Products and their Elimination","qno":115,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Excretory Products and their Elimination","type":"mcq","question":"A drug is given that blocks the transport of electrolytes out of the ascending limb of Henle's loop, while all other segments work normally. Which outcome is most likely?","image":"","options":["Filtrate in the descending limb loses more water, so the urine becomes more concentrated","The medullary osmotic gradient falls, so less water is reabsorbed from the collecting duct and the urine is less concentrated","Reabsorption of glucose and amino acids in the PCT stops completely","The collecting duct stops secreting $K^+$, so urine becomes highly concentrated"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The ascending limb transports NaCl into the medullary interstitium, and this builds the 300 to 1200 $mOsmol\\,L^{-1}$ gradient.\nIf this transport is blocked, the interstitium becomes less hypertonic.\nWith a weaker gradient, less water passes out of the descending limb and the collecting duct, so the urine becomes less concentrated.\nOption (1) is wrong because a weaker gradient draws out less water, not more.\nPCT glucose reabsorption and collecting duct K+ secretion are unaffected by this drug.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 16 · Excretory Products and their Elimination","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-EXCRETORY-116","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Excretory Products and their Elimination","qno":116,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Excretory Products and their Elimination","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: The juxta glomerular apparatus is a sensitive region formed by cellular modifications in the distal convoluted tubule and the afferent arteriole at their point of contact.\nStatement II: A rise in GFR activates the JG cells to release renin, which further increases glomerular blood flow.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct: NCERT describes the JGA as cellular modifications of the DCT and the afferent arteriole where they touch.\nStatement II is incorrect: it is a fall in GFR that activates the JG cells to release renin.\nRenin then stimulates glomerular blood flow and brings GFR back to normal.\nReleasing renin on a rise in GFR would push GFR even further from normal, so it is not autoregulation.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 16 · Excretory Products and their Elimination","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-EXCRETORY-117","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Excretory Products and their Elimination","qno":117,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Excretory Products and their Elimination","type":"mcq","question":"The minute spaces called filtration slits or slit pores, through which blood is finely filtered in the kidney, are left between which cells?","image":"","options":["Podocytes of the Bowman's capsule epithelium","Endothelial cells of the efferent arteriole","Juxtaglomerular cells of the afferent arteriole","Cuboidal cells of the distal convoluted tubule"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The epithelial cells of Bowman's capsule are called podocytes.\nThey are arranged in an intricate way that leaves minute gaps, the filtration slits or slit pores.\nJG cells release renin and do not form slits. The efferent arteriole carries blood away from the glomerulus. The DCT is a reabsorbing segment.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 16 · Excretory Products and their Elimination","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-EXCRETORY-118","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Excretory Products and their Elimination","qno":118,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Excretory Products and their Elimination","type":"mcq","question":"Filtration of the blood takes place at","image":"","options":["PCT","DCT","Collecting ducts","Malpighian body"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Ultrafiltration of blood is the first step in urine formation. It occurs across the glomerular capillaries into Bowman's capsule.\nThe glomerulus and Bowman's capsule together form the Malpighian body (renal corpuscle).\nThe PCT and DCT carry out reabsorption and secretion, not filtration.\nThe collecting ducts concentrate urine by reabsorbing water.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Exemplar Biology Class 11, Ch 19 (MCQ Q2)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 16 · Excretory Products and their Elimination","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-EXCRETORY-119","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Excretory Products and their Elimination","qno":119,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Excretory Products and their Elimination","type":"mcq","question":"Which part of the renal tubular system allows small amounts of urea to pass into the medullary interstitium to help keep up its osmolarity?","image":"","options":["Collecting duct","Proximal convoluted tubule","Bowman's capsule","Distal convoluted tubule"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"NCERT states that the collecting duct allows small amounts of urea to pass into the medullary interstitium to maintain its osmolarity.\nThe collecting duct extends from the cortex into the inner medulla. This is where urea contributes, along with NaCl, to the osmotic gradient.\nThe DCT is concerned with conditional Na+ and water reabsorption and ion secretion, not urea passage, so option (4) is wrong.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 16 · Excretory Products and their Elimination","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-EXCRETORY-120","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Excretory Products and their Elimination","qno":120,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Excretory Products and their Elimination","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Amphioxus, although a chordate, has protonephridia (flame cells) as its excretory structures.\nReason (R): Protonephridia are found exclusively in non-chordate animals.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Assertion: NCERT names the cephalochordate Amphioxus among animals with protonephridia, so A is true.\nReason: protonephridia occur in Amphioxus, which is a chordate. They are therefore not exclusive to non-chordates, so R is false.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 16 · Excretory Products and their Elimination","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-EXCRETORY-121","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Excretory Products and their Elimination","qno":121,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Excretory Products and their Elimination","type":"mcq","question":"Suppose a drug blocks the movement of electrolytes out of the ascending limb of Henle's loop. The rest of the nephron is unaffected. Which outcome is most likely?","image":"","options":["The medullary osmotic gradient is reduced, so less water is reabsorbed from the collecting duct and a larger volume of less concentrated urine is formed","The medullary osmotic gradient increases, so a smaller volume of more concentrated urine is formed","The descending limb becomes impermeable to water, so the filtrate is diluted as it moves down","Urine concentration is unchanged because the collecting duct reabsorbs water independently of the medullary gradient"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The ascending limb transports electrolytes, mainly NaCl, into the medullary interstitium. This builds up the osmotic gradient.\nIf this transport is blocked, interstitial osmolarity falls.\nWater leaves the collecting duct easily only because of this gradient.\nWith a weaker gradient, less water is reabsorbed, so urine becomes more dilute and greater in volume.\nOption (2) predicts the opposite effect.\nOption (4) ignores the gradient's role in water reabsorption.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 16 · Excretory Products and their Elimination","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-EXCRETORY-122","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Excretory Products and their Elimination","qno":122,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Excretory Products and their Elimination","type":"mcq","question":"According to NCERT, roughly how much carbon dioxide do human lungs remove per minute?","image":"","options":["About 20 mL","About 200 mL","About 1200 mL","About 2 L"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT states that the lungs remove large amounts of CO$_2$, about 200 mL per minute. They also remove significant quantities of water every day.\nThe value 1200 mL is a tempting distractor. It is the volume of blood filtered by the kidneys per minute, not the CO$_2$ output of the lungs.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 16 · Excretory Products and their Elimination","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-EXCRETORY-123","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Excretory Products and their Elimination","qno":123,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Excretory Products and their Elimination","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following is removed from our body by lungs?","image":"","options":["$CO_2$ only","$H_2O$ only","$CO_2$ and $H_2O$","Ammonia"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"NCERT notes that the lungs remove large amounts of $CO_2$, about 200 mL per minute.\nThe lungs also lose significant quantities of water as vapour in exhaled air every day.\nSo the lungs remove both $CO_2$ and $H_2O$.\nOptions (1) and (2) each give only one of the two products.\nAmmonia is excreted by aquatic animals through body surfaces or gills, not by human lungs.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Exemplar Biology Class 11, Ch 19 (MCQ Q4)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 16 · Excretory Products and their Elimination","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-EXCRETORY-124","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Excretory Products and their Elimination","qno":124,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Excretory Products and their Elimination","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): An excessive loss of fluid from the body leads to increased release of antidiuretic hormone (ADH) from the neurohypophysis.\nReason (R): ADH causes dilation of blood vessels and thereby lowers blood pressure.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A is true. Fluid loss activates osmoreceptors, which stimulate the hypothalamus to release ADH (vasopressin) from the neurohypophysis.\nR is false. ADH has a constrictory effect on blood vessels, so it raises blood pressure and GFR.\nVasodilation that lowers blood pressure is the action of ANF, not ADH.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 16 · Excretory Products and their Elimination","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-EXCRETORY-125","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Excretory Products and their Elimination","qno":125,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Excretory Products and their Elimination","type":"mcq","question":"Which sequence correctly shows the events that follow a fall in GFR?","image":"","options":["Renin release → Angiotensin II → Angiotensin I → Aldosterone → Na+ reabsorption","Aldosterone release → Renin release → Angiotensin I → Angiotensin II → Rise in GFR","Renin release → Angiotensinogen → Angiotensin I → ADH release → Water loss","Renin release → Angiotensin I → Angiotensin II → Aldosterone → Na+ and water reabsorption"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"A fall in GFR activates the JG cells to release renin.\nRenin converts angiotensinogen into angiotensin I, which is then converted into angiotensin II.\nAngiotensin II stimulates the adrenal cortex to release aldosterone.\nAldosterone causes Na+ and water reabsorption from the distal tubule, which raises blood pressure and GFR.\nOption (1) is wrong because it reverses angiotensin I and angiotensin II.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 16 · Excretory Products and their Elimination","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-EXCRETORY-126","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Excretory Products and their Elimination","qno":126,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Excretory Products and their Elimination","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II:\nList-I\n(A) Hilum\n(B) Renal pelvis\n(C) Calyces\n(D) Medullary pyramids\nList-II\n(I) Projections of the funnel-shaped space inner to the hilum\n(II) Conical masses of the inner zone of the kidney\n(III) Notch on the inner concave surface of the kidney\n(IV) Broad funnel-shaped space inner to the hilum\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II","A-IV, B-III, C-II, D-I","A-III, B-I, C-IV, D-II","A-II, B-IV, C-I, D-III"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Hilum: the notch at the centre of the inner concave surface (III).\nRenal pelvis: the broad funnel-shaped space inner to the hilum (IV).\nCalyces: projections of the renal pelvis (I).\nMedullary pyramids: conical masses into which the medulla is divided (II).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 16 · Excretory Products and their Elimination","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-EXCRETORY-127","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Excretory Products and their Elimination","qno":127,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Excretory Products and their Elimination","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (part of nephron) with List-II (feature):\nList-I\nA. Proximal convoluted tubule\nB. Descending limb of Henle's loop\nC. Ascending limb of Henle's loop\nD. Collecting duct\nList-II\n(i) Impermeable to water but allows transport of electrolytes\n(ii) Permeable to water but almost impermeable to electrolytes\n(iii) Allows small amounts of urea into the medullary interstitium\n(iv) Reabsorbs 70-80 per cent of electrolytes and water","image":"","options":["A-(iv), B-(i), C-(ii), D-(iii)","A-(iii), B-(ii), C-(i), D-(iv)","A-(iv), B-(ii), C-(i), D-(iii)","A-(ii), B-(iv), C-(iii), D-(i)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A. The PCT reabsorbs nearly all essential nutrients and 70-80% of electrolytes and water, so A-(iv).\nB. The descending limb lets water out but holds back electrolytes, so B-(ii).\nC. The ascending limb is impermeable to water but moves electrolytes, so C-(i).\nD. The collecting duct passes small amounts of urea into the medullary interstitium to maintain osmolarity, so D-(iii).\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 16 · Excretory Products and their Elimination","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-EXCRETORY-128","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Excretory Products and their Elimination","qno":128,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Excretory Products and their Elimination","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following animals possess protonephridia (flame cells) as excretory structures?\nPlanaria, rotifer, Amphioxus, cockroach, prawn, frog","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Planaria (a flatworm), rotifers and Amphioxus have protonephridia, which makes 3.\nThe cockroach has Malpighian tubules.\nThe prawn has antennal (green) glands.\nThe frog, a vertebrate, has kidneys.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 16 · Excretory Products and their Elimination","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-EXCRETORY-129","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Excretory Products and their Elimination","qno":129,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Excretory Products and their Elimination","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Inner to the hilum of the kidney lies a broad funnel-shaped space called the renal pelvis.\nStatement II: The outer layer of the kidney is the medulla, which encloses the cortex.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct: the renal pelvis is the broad funnel-shaped space inner to the hilum, and it bears calyces.\nStatement II is incorrect: the outer layer of the kidney is a tough capsule.\nInside the capsule, the cortex is the outer zone and the medulla is the inner zone.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 16 · Excretory Products and their Elimination","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-EXCRETORY-130","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Excretory Products and their Elimination","qno":130,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Excretory Products and their Elimination","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I\n(a) Uremia\n(b) Renal calculi\n(c) Glomerulonephritis\n(d) Haemodialysis\nList-II\n(i) Inflammation of glomeruli\n(ii) Accumulation of urea in blood\n(iii) Removal of urea from blood using an artificial kidney\n(iv) Insoluble mass of crystallised salts such as oxalates in the kidney\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["a-ii, b-i, c-iv, d-iii","a-iii, b-iv, c-i, d-ii","a-ii, b-iv, c-i, d-iii","a-iv, b-ii, c-iii, d-i"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(a) Uremia is the accumulation of urea in blood, so a-ii.\n(b) Renal calculi are stones made of crystallised salts such as oxalates, so b-iv.\n(c) Glomerulonephritis is inflammation of the glomeruli, so c-i.\n(d) Haemodialysis clears urea from blood using an artificial kidney, so d-iii.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 16 · Excretory Products and their Elimination","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-EXCRETORY-131","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Excretory Products and their Elimination","qno":131,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Excretory Products and their Elimination","type":"mcq","question":"Arrange the following steps of haemodialysis in the correct order:\n(i) Anti-heparin is added to the cleared blood\n(ii) Blood is drained from a convenient artery\n(iii) Blood flows through a coiled cellophane tube bathed in dialysing fluid\n(iv) Heparin is added to the blood\n(v) Blood is returned to the body through a vein","image":"","options":["(ii) → (iv) → (iii) → (i) → (v)","(iv) → (ii) → (iii) → (v) → (i)","(ii) → (iii) → (iv) → (i) → (v)","(ii) → (iv) → (i) → (iii) → (v)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Step 1: blood is drained from a convenient artery (ii).\nStep 2: heparin is added so that the blood does not clot inside the unit (iv).\nStep 3: the blood passes through the coiled cellophane tube, where nitrogenous wastes diffuse out (iii).\nStep 4: anti-heparin is added to restore normal clotting ability (i).\nStep 5: the cleared blood is returned to the body through a vein (v).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 16 · Excretory Products and their Elimination","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-EXCRETORY-132","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Excretory Products and their Elimination","qno":132,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Excretory Products and their Elimination","type":"mcq","question":"Which one of the following statements is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["Although the bladder wall is made of smooth muscle, its emptying is triggered through motor messages from the CNS","Ketonuria and glycosuria are both indicative of diabetes mellitus","The release of urine is triggered when chemoreceptors in the bladder wall detect a high urea concentration in the stored urine","An adult human excretes, on an average, 1 to 1.5 litres of urine per day"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: the CNS passes on motor messages that make the bladder's smooth muscles contract.\n(2) Correct: NCERT states that glucose and ketone bodies in urine indicate diabetes mellitus.\n(3) Incorrect: the trigger is mechanical, not chemical. Stretching of the filling bladder activates stretch receptors; urea concentration has no role.\n(4) Correct: the average daily urine output is 1 to 1.5 litres.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 16 · Excretory Products and their Elimination","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-EXCRETORY-133","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Excretory Products and their Elimination","qno":133,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Excretory Products and their Elimination","type":"mcq","question":"Match the abnormal conditions given in Column A with their explanations given in Column B and Choose the correct option\nColumn A — Column B\nA. Glycosuria — i. Accumulation of uric acid in joints\nB. Renal calculi — ii. Inflammation in glomeruli\nC. Glomerular nephritis — iii. Mass of crystallised salts within the kidney\nD. Gout — iv. presence of glucose in urine","image":"","options":["A-i, B-iii, C-ii, D-iv","A-iii, B-ii, C-iv, D-i","A-iv, B-iii, C-ii, D-i","A-iv, B-ii, C-iii, D-i"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A. Glycosuria is the presence of glucose in the urine → iv.\nB. Renal calculi are stones, i.e. masses of crystallised salts formed within the kidney → iii.\nC. Glomerular nephritis is inflammation of the glomeruli → ii.\nD. Gout is the accumulation of uric acid crystals in the joints → i.\nThis gives A-iv, B-iii, C-ii, D-i.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Exemplar Biology Class 11, Ch 19 (MCQ Q13)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 16 · Excretory Products and their Elimination","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-EXCRETORY-134","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Excretory Products and their Elimination","qno":134,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Excretory Products and their Elimination","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following nephron segments secrete $H^+$ ions into the filtrate to help maintain acid-base balance?\n(i) Proximal convoluted tubule\n(ii) Descending limb of Henle's loop\n(iii) Ascending limb of Henle's loop\n(iv) Distal convoluted tubule\n(v) Collecting duct","image":"","options":["Two","Four","Three","Five"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) PCT: yes, it secretes H+ and NH3 and absorbs $HCO_3^-$.\n(ii) Descending limb: no, it is only a site of water movement.\n(iii) Ascending limb: no, it transports electrolytes out but does not secrete H+.\n(iv) DCT: yes, it secretes H+, K+ and NH3.\n(v) Collecting duct: yes, it selectively secretes H+ and K+.\nSo three segments secrete H+.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 16 · Excretory Products and their Elimination","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-EXCRETORY-135","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Excretory Products and their Elimination","qno":135,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Excretory Products and their Elimination","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): In bony fishes, the kidneys play no significant role in removing ammonia.\nReason (R): Ammonia is readily soluble, so it is excreted as ammonium ions by diffusion across body surfaces or gill surfaces.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. NCERT states that in ammonotelic animals, such as many bony fishes, the kidneys do not play any significant role in removing ammonia.\nR is true. Ammonia is readily soluble, so it diffuses out as $NH_4^+$ ions across body surfaces or, in fish, across the gills.\nR explains A. Because ammonia diffuses out directly through these surfaces, the kidneys are not needed to remove it.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 16 · Excretory Products and their Elimination","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-EXCRETORY-136","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Excretory Products and their Elimination","qno":136,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Excretory Products and their Elimination","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II:\nList-I\n(a) ADH\n(b) Renin\n(c) Angiotensin II\n(d) ANF\nList-II\n(i) Released from the atria; causes vasodilation\n(ii) Powerful vasoconstrictor that also activates the adrenal cortex\n(iii) Released by JG cells; converts angiotensinogen\n(iv) Released from the neurohypophysis; promotes water reabsorption\nChoose the correct option:","image":"","options":["(a)-(iv), (b)-(iii), (c)-(ii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(ii), (c)-(iii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(i), (b)-(iii), (c)-(ii), (d)-(iv)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(a) ADH is released from the neurohypophysis and promotes water reabsorption, so (a)-(iv).\n(b) Renin is released by JG cells and converts angiotensinogen, so (b)-(iii).\n(c) Angiotensin II is a strong vasoconstrictor and stimulates the adrenal cortex to release aldosterone, so (c)-(ii).\n(d) ANF is released from the atria and causes vasodilation, so (d)-(i).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 16 · Excretory Products and their Elimination","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-EXCRETORY-137","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Excretory Products and their Elimination","qno":137,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Excretory Products and their Elimination","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II:\nList-I\n(a) ADH\n(b) Renin\n(c) Angiotensin II\n(d) ANF\nList-II\n(i) Causes vasodilation and lowers blood pressure\n(ii) Converts angiotensinogen to angiotensin I\n(iii) Stimulates the adrenal cortex to release aldosterone\n(iv) Facilitates water reabsorption from the latter parts of the tubule\nChoose the correct option:","image":"","options":["a-(iv), b-(ii), c-(iii), d-(i)","a-(ii), b-(iv), c-(i), d-(iii)","a-(iv), b-(iii), c-(ii), d-(i)","a-(i), b-(ii), c-(iii), d-(iv)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(a) ADH promotes water reabsorption from the latter parts of the tubule and prevents diuresis: (iv).\n(b) Renin converts angiotensinogen to angiotensin I: (ii).\n(c) Angiotensin II activates the adrenal cortex to release aldosterone: (iii).\n(d) ANF causes vasodilation and lowers blood pressure: (i).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 16 · Excretory Products and their Elimination","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-EXCRETORY-138","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Excretory Products and their Elimination","qno":138,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Excretory Products and their Elimination","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following structures of a juxtamedullary nephron lie in the cortical region of the kidney?\nGlomerulus; Bowman's capsule; Proximal convoluted tubule; Distal convoluted tubule; Loop of Henle; Vasa recta","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The Malpighian corpuscle lies in the cortex. This means the glomerulus and Bowman's capsule are both cortical (2).\nThe PCT and DCT are also situated in the cortex (4).\nIn juxtamedullary nephrons, the loop of Henle runs deep into the medulla, so it is not cortical.\nThe vasa recta runs parallel to the loop of Henle, so it also lies in the medulla.\nCortical structures = 4.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 16 · Excretory Products and their Elimination","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-EXCRETORY-139","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Excretory Products and their Elimination","qno":139,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Excretory Products and their Elimination","type":"mcq","question":"The Malpighian body (renal corpuscle) of a nephron consists of:","image":"","options":["Glomerulus and proximal convoluted tubule","Bowman's capsule and Henle's loop","Glomerulus and Bowman's capsule","Glomerulus and juxtaglomerular apparatus"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The renal tubule begins as a double-walled, cup-like Bowman's capsule that encloses the glomerulus.\nThe glomerulus together with Bowman's capsule is called the Malpighian body, or renal corpuscle.\nThe PCT and Henle's loop are later parts of the renal tubule, not of the corpuscle.\nThe JGA is a region where the DCT contacts the afferent arteriole, so it is also not part of the corpuscle.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 16 · Excretory Products and their Elimination","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-EXCRETORY-140","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Excretory Products and their Elimination","qno":140,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Excretory Products and their Elimination","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): In juxtamedullary nephrons, the Malpighian corpuscle, PCT and DCT are located in the renal cortex.\nReason (R): In juxtamedullary nephrons, the loop of Henle is very long and runs deep into the medulla, accompanied by a well-developed vasa recta.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"A is true. In every nephron, including juxtamedullary ones, the Malpighian corpuscle, PCT and DCT lie in the cortex.\nR is true. Juxtamedullary nephrons have long loops of Henle that dip deep into the medulla. The vasa recta is well developed in them, whereas it is absent or highly reduced in cortical nephrons.\nHowever, the depth of the loop does not explain why the corpuscle and convoluted tubules are cortical. That is the common plan of all nephrons, so R does not explain A.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 16 · Excretory Products and their Elimination","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-EXCRETORY-141","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Excretory Products and their Elimination","qno":141,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Excretory Products and their Elimination","type":"mcq","question":"In the human kidney, the counter current mechanism maintains an increasing osmolarity of the medullary interstitium from the cortex towards the inner medulla. What is the approximate osmolarity of the interstitium in the inner medulla?","image":"","options":["300 $mOsmol\\,L^{-1}$","600 $mOsmol\\,L^{-1}$","1200 $mOsmol\\,L^{-1}$","2400 $mOsmol\\,L^{-1}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"NCERT fact: the interstitial osmolarity rises from about 300 $mOsmol\\,L^{-1}$ in the cortex to about 1200 $mOsmol\\,L^{-1}$ in the inner medulla.\nOption (1) is the cortical value, not the inner medullary value.\nOption (2) is only an intermediate value along the gradient.\nOption (4) is double the value given in NCERT.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 16 · Excretory Products and their Elimination","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-EXCRETORY-142","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Excretory Products and their Elimination","qno":142,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Excretory Products and their Elimination","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements regarding the internal organisation of the human kidney is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["The renal columns between the medullary pyramids are made of cortical tissue","Malpighian corpuscles of the nephrons are located in the medullary pyramids","Loops of Henle of juxta medullary nephrons run deep into the medulla","Collecting ducts open into the renal pelvis through the medullary pyramids in the calyces"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: the cortex extends between the pyramids as the Columns of Bertini.\n(2) Incorrect: Malpighian corpuscles, PCT and DCT lie in the cortex. Only the loop of Henle dips into the medulla.\n(3) Correct: juxta medullary nephrons have long loops that run deep into the medulla.\n(4) Correct: this matches NCERT's description of collecting ducts opening into the pelvis through the pyramids in the calyces.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 16 · Excretory Products and their Elimination","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-EXCRETORY-143","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Excretory Products and their Elimination","qno":143,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Excretory Products and their Elimination","type":"mcq","question":"What is the driving force for the filtration of blood in the glomerulus?","image":"","options":["Osmotic pressure of plasma proteins","Glomerular capillary blood pressure","Active pumping of plasma by podocytes","Hydrostatic pressure of fluid in Bowman's capsule"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT states that the glomerular capillary blood pressure causes filtration of blood through the filtration membrane.\nFiltration is therefore a passive, pressure-driven process.\nPodocytes do not pump fluid. They only form the filtration slits.\nPlasma protein osmotic pressure and capsular fluid pressure do not push fluid out of the capillaries.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 16 · Excretory Products and their Elimination","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-EXCRETORY-144","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Excretory Products and their Elimination","qno":144,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Excretory Products and their Elimination","type":"mcq","question":"Which excretory structures are found in Planaria, a flatworm?","image":"","options":["Nephridia","Flame cells (protonephridia)","Malpighian tubules","Green glands"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT lists protonephridia, or flame cells, as the excretory structures of Platyhelminthes such as Planaria.\nNephridia occur in earthworms and other annelids.\nMalpighian tubules occur in insects, and green glands occur in crustaceans.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 16 · Excretory Products and their Elimination","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-EXCRETORY-145","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Excretory Products and their Elimination","qno":145,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Excretory Products and their Elimination","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (parts of the human kidney) with List-II (their descriptions) and choose the correct option.\nList-I:\n(a) Hilum\n(b) Renal pelvis\n(c) Medullary pyramids\n(d) Columns of Bertini\nList-II:\n(i) Conical masses of the medulla projecting into the calyces\n(ii) Extensions of the cortex lying between the medullary pyramids\n(iii) Notch on the inner concave surface through which the ureter, blood vessels and nerves enter\n(iv) Broad funnel-shaped space with projections called calyces","image":"","options":["(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(iii), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(ii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(iii), (c)-(ii), (d)-(i)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(a) Hilum: the notch at the centre of the inner concave surface, through which the ureter, vessels and nerves enter. So (a)-(iii).\n(b) Renal pelvis: lies inner to the hilum. It is a broad funnel-shaped space bearing calyces. So (b)-(iv).\n(c) Medullary pyramids: conical masses of the medulla that project into the calyces. So (c)-(i).\n(d) Columns of Bertini: the cortex extending between the pyramids as renal columns. So (d)-(ii).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 16 · Excretory Products and their Elimination","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-EXCRETORY-146","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Excretory Products and their Elimination","qno":146,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Excretory Products and their Elimination","type":"mcq","question":"The signal that finally leads to the release of urine is initiated by:","image":"","options":["A rise in urea concentration detected by chemoreceptors in the ureter","Stretching of the urinary bladder wall as it fills with urine","Release of ADH from the neurohypophysis","Contraction of the urethral sphincter"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"As the bladder fills with urine, its wall stretches.\nStretch receptors in the bladder wall then send signals to the CNS. This is how the micturition signal starts.\nADH regulates water reabsorption in the tubules, not urine release.\nDuring micturition the urethral sphincter relaxes; it does not contract.\nNo chemoreceptor in the ureter is involved.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 16 · Excretory Products and their Elimination","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-EXCRETORY-147","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Excretory Products and their Elimination","qno":147,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Excretory Products and their Elimination","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about the functions of renal tubule segments are correct?\n(i) The descending limb of Henle's loop is permeable to water but almost impermeable to electrolytes.\n(ii) The ascending limb of Henle's loop is impermeable to water.\n(iii) Reabsorption is maximum in the ascending limb of Henle's loop.\n(iv) The collecting duct allows small amounts of urea into the medullary interstitium.\n(v) PCT reabsorbs about 70-80 per cent of electrolytes and water.\n(vi) DCT secretes $H^+$ and $K^+$ but cannot reabsorb $HCO_3^-$.","image":"","options":["Two","Three","Four","Five"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: water leaves the descending limb, so the filtrate becomes concentrated.\n(ii) Correct: the ascending limb lets only electrolytes pass, so the filtrate is diluted.\n(iii) Incorrect: reabsorption is minimum in the ascending limb.\n(iv) Correct: this urea helps keep up the medullary osmolarity.\n(v) Correct: the PCT is the major site of reabsorption.\n(vi) Incorrect: the DCT can reabsorb $HCO_3^-$.\nFour statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 16 · Excretory Products and their Elimination","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-EXCRETORY-148","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Excretory Products and their Elimination","qno":148,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Excretory Products and their Elimination","type":"mcq","question":"In a typical nephron of the human kidney, which group of parts lies in the cortical region?","image":"","options":["Malpighian corpuscle, loop of Henle and collecting duct","Malpighian corpuscle, PCT and DCT","PCT, loop of Henle and vasa recta","Glomerulus, DCT and renal pelvis"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"According to NCERT, the Malpighian corpuscle, the PCT and the DCT lie in the cortex.\nThe loop of Henle dips into the medulla. In cortical nephrons it dips only slightly; in juxtamedullary nephrons it dips deeply.\nThe collecting duct runs from the cortex into the inner medulla.\nThe renal pelvis is a funnel-shaped space inner to the hilum, not part of the cortex.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 16 · Excretory Products and their Elimination","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-EXCRETORY-149","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Excretory Products and their Elimination","qno":149,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Excretory Products and their Elimination","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Birds and reptiles excrete their nitrogenous wastes mainly as uric acid.\nReason (R): Uric acid is the least toxic nitrogenous waste and can be eliminated with minimum loss of water.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. Reptiles, birds, land snails and insects are uricotelic, and they pass uric acid out as a pellet or paste.\nR is true. Uric acid is the least toxic nitrogenous waste and needs very little water for its removal.\nR explains A. Excreting uric acid conserves water, which suits these animals.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 16 · Excretory Products and their Elimination","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-EXCRETORY-150","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Excretory Products and their Elimination","qno":150,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Excretory Products and their Elimination","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements is correct?","image":"","options":["ADH – prevents conversion of angiotensinogen in blood to angiotensin","Aldosterone – facilitates water reabsorption","ANF – enhances sodium reabsorption","Renin – causes vasodilation"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(1) Incorrect: ADH promotes water reabsorption. It is renin, not ADH, that acts on angiotensinogen.\n(2) Correct: aldosterone makes the distal tubule reabsorb Na$^{+}$, and water follows. This raises blood volume and pressure.\n(3) Incorrect: ANF opposes the RAAS, so it reduces sodium reabsorption.\n(4) Incorrect: renin forms angiotensin, which becomes angiotensin II, a vasoconstrictor. Vasodilation is caused by ANF.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Exemplar Biology Class 11, Ch 19 (MCQ Q3)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 16 · Excretory Products and their Elimination","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-EXCRETORY-151","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Excretory Products and their Elimination","qno":151,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Excretory Products and their Elimination","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about glomerular filtration is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["The filtrate reaches the lumen of Bowman's capsule after passing through the filtration slits between podocytes","The glomerulus is a tuft of capillaries formed by the afferent arteriole, a fine branch of the renal artery","A GFR of 125 ml per minute corresponds to roughly 180 litres of filtrate per day","Filtration is selective, so glucose and amino acids are retained in the blood and do not enter the filtrate"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: filtrate passes through the slit pores between podocytes into Bowman's capsule.\n(2) Correct: the afferent arteriole, a branch of the renal artery, forms the glomerulus.\n(3) Correct: 125 ml × 1440 min = 180,000 ml, which is about 180 L per day.\n(4) Incorrect: filtration is non-selective. Glucose and amino acids do enter the filtrate. They are actively reabsorbed later, mainly in the PCT.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 16 · Excretory Products and their Elimination","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-EXCRETORY-152","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Excretory Products and their Elimination","qno":152,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Excretory Products and their Elimination","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Sweat helps in the removal of some wastes such as urea and NaCl from the body.\nReason (R): The primary function of sweat is to produce a cooling effect on the body surface.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"A is true: sweat contains NaCl, small amounts of urea and lactic acid, so it helps remove these wastes.\nR is true: NCERT states that the primary function of sweat is cooling the body surface.\nHowever, cooling does not explain why wastes are removed. Excretion is only a secondary role of sweat.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 16 · Excretory Products and their Elimination","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-EXCRETORY-153","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Excretory Products and their Elimination","qno":153,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Excretory Products and their Elimination","type":"mcq","question":"The renal columns called Columns of Bertini are","image":"","options":["projections of the renal pelvis into the medulla","conical masses of medulla projecting into the calyces","extensions of the cortex between the medullary pyramids","folds of the outer tough capsule around the hilum"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The cortex extends in between the medullary pyramids as renal columns, which are called Columns of Bertini.\nOption (2) describes the medullary pyramids.\nOption (1) describes the calyces, which are projections of the renal pelvis.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 16 · Excretory Products and their Elimination","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-EXCRETORY-154","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Excretory Products and their Elimination","qno":154,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Excretory Products and their Elimination","type":"mcq","question":"The pH of human urine is approximately","image":"","options":["6.5","7","6","7.5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Human urine is a light yellow, watery fluid that is slightly acidic.\nNCERT gives its pH as about 6.0.\nA value of 7 or 7.5 would mean neutral or alkaline urine, so those options do not fit.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Exemplar Biology Class 11, Ch 19 (MCQ Q5)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 16 · Excretory Products and their Elimination","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-EXCRETORY-155","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Excretory Products and their Elimination","qno":155,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Excretory Products and their Elimination","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about antidiuretic hormone (ADH) is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["An increase in body fluid volume switches off the osmoreceptors and suppresses ADH release.","ADH constricts blood vessels, which can raise blood pressure and GFR.","ADH acts chiefly on the proximal convoluted tubule to increase water reabsorption.","ADH prevents diuresis by increasing water reabsorption."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: suppressing ADH when body fluid volume rises completes the feedback loop.\n(2) Correct: the vasoconstriction caused by ADH raises blood pressure, which increases glomerular blood flow and GFR.\n(3) Incorrect: ADH promotes water reabsorption from the latter parts of the tubule, not the PCT. Water reabsorption in the PCT is passive and not under ADH control.\n(4) Correct: increasing water reabsorption is how ADH prevents diuresis.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 16 · Excretory Products and their Elimination","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-EXCRETORY-156","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Excretory Products and their Elimination","qno":156,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Excretory Products and their Elimination","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following substances are named in NCERT as being secreted by the liver in bile?\nBilirubin, biliverdin, cholesterol, degraded steroid hormones, vitamins, lactic acid, waxes","image":"","options":["Three","Four","Five","Six"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"NCERT lists the following bile substances: bilirubin, biliverdin, cholesterol, degraded steroid hormones, vitamins and drugs.\nFrom the given list, five qualify: bilirubin, biliverdin, cholesterol, degraded steroid hormones and vitamins.\nLactic acid is not a bile substance. It is a component of sweat.\nWaxes are not bile substances either. They are eliminated in sebum.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 16 · Excretory Products and their Elimination","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-EXCRETORY-157","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Excretory Products and their Elimination","qno":157,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Excretory Products and their Elimination","type":"mcq","question":"The following substances are the excretory products in animals. Choose the least toxic form among them.","image":"","options":["Urea","Uric acid","Ammonia","Carbon dioxide"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Toxicity of nitrogenous wastes falls in the order ammonia > urea > uric acid.\nUric acid is the least toxic and is nearly insoluble. It can be excreted as a paste or pellet with minimal water loss, so it suits birds, reptiles and land snails.\nAmmonia is the most toxic and needs a lot of water to remove. Urea is intermediate.\n$CO_2$ is not a nitrogenous waste, so it is not part of this comparison.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Exemplar Biology Class 11, Ch 19 (MCQ Q1)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 16 · Excretory Products and their Elimination","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-EXCRETORY-158","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Excretory Products and their Elimination","qno":158,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Excretory Products and their Elimination","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (structure) with List-II (role in counter current mechanism):\nList-I\n(a) Ascending limb of Henle's loop\n(b) Descending limb of vasa recta\n(c) Ascending limb of vasa recta\n(d) Collecting tubule\nList-II\n(i) Returns NaCl to the medullary interstitium\n(ii) Takes up NaCl given out by the loop of Henle\n(iii) Transports NaCl out of the filtrate into the interstitium\n(iv) Transports urea back to the interstitium","image":"","options":["a-iii, b-ii, c-i, d-iv","a-ii, b-iii, c-iv, d-i","a-iii, b-i, c-ii, d-iv","a-iv, b-ii, c-i, d-iii"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(a) The ascending limb of Henle's loop transports NaCl out of the filtrate, so (a)-(iii).\n(b) The descending vasa recta receives this NaCl by exchange, so (b)-(ii).\n(c) The ascending vasa recta returns the NaCl to the interstitium, so (c)-(i).\n(d) The collecting tubule passes urea back into the interstitium, so (d)-(iv).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 16 · Excretory Products and their Elimination","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-EXCRETORY-159","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Excretory Products and their Elimination","qno":159,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Excretory Products and their Elimination","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following tend to INCREASE the glomerular filtration rate (GFR)?\n(i) Vasoconstriction caused by ADH\n(ii) Angiotensin II acting on blood vessels\n(iii) Aldosterone-induced reabsorption of Na+ and water\n(iv) Vasodilation caused by ANF","image":"","options":["One","Two","Three","Four"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Increases GFR: ADH constricts blood vessels, which raises blood pressure and glomerular blood flow.\n(ii) Increases GFR: angiotensin II raises glomerular blood pressure.\n(iii) Increases GFR: retaining Na+ and water raises blood pressure.\n(iv) Does not increase GFR: ANF lowers blood pressure and opposes the renin-angiotensin mechanism.\nSo three of the four increase GFR.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 16 · Excretory Products and their Elimination","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-EXCRETORY-160","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Excretory Products and their Elimination","qno":160,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Excretory Products and their Elimination","type":"mcq","question":"Routine urine analysis of a patient shows glucose and ketone bodies in the urine. These findings are most indicative of:","image":"","options":["Diabetes insipidus","Uremia","Diabetes mellitus","Renal calculi"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Glucose in urine is called glycosuria, and ketone bodies in urine is called ketonuria.\nNCERT states that both are indicative of diabetes mellitus.\nDiabetes insipidus is a tempting choice because of the similar name. However, it relates to ADH and does not cause glucose in urine.\nUremia is accumulation of urea in blood.\nRenal calculi are stones of crystallised salts formed in the kidney.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 16 · Excretory Products and their Elimination","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-EXCRETORY-161","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Excretory Products and their Elimination","qno":161,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Excretory Products and their Elimination","type":"mcq","question":"A person loses a large volume of blood in an accident, so blood volume and glomerular blood pressure fall sharply. Which of the following responses would NOT be expected as part of the body's attempt to restore GFR and blood pressure?","image":"","options":["Release of renin from JG cells","Increased release of ADH from the neurohypophysis","Increased release of aldosterone from the adrenal cortex","Increased release of Atrial Natriuretic Factor (ANF)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"A fall in glomerular blood pressure activates JG cells to release renin. The resulting angiotensin II constricts blood vessels and also triggers aldosterone release, which increases Na+ and water reabsorption. So (1) and (3) are expected. \nFluid loss stimulates osmoreceptors, which leads to ADH release and water conservation. So (2) is expected. \nANF is released when blood flow to the atria increases. It causes vasodilation and lowers blood pressure, which is the opposite of what the body needs here. \nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 16 · Excretory Products and their Elimination","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-EXCRETORY-162","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Excretory Products and their Elimination","qno":162,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Excretory Products and their Elimination","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Juxtamedullary nephrons contribute far more to the production of concentrated urine than cortical nephrons.\nReason (R): In juxtamedullary nephrons the loop of Henle runs deep into the medulla and is accompanied by a well-developed vasa recta, which is absent or highly reduced in cortical nephrons.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"R is true. NCERT states that juxtamedullary nephrons have long loops of Henle running deep into the medulla, and that vasa recta is absent or highly reduced in cortical nephrons.\nA is true. Concentrating urine depends on the counter current mechanism between Henle's loop and vasa recta, which keeps the medullary gradient (300 to 1200 mOsmol/L).\nOnly long loops with vasa recta can build and keep this gradient, so R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 16 · Excretory Products and their Elimination","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-EXCRETORY-163","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Excretory Products and their Elimination","qno":163,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Excretory Products and their Elimination","type":"mcq","question":"We can produce a concentrated/ dilute urine. This is facilitated by a special mechanism. Identify the mechanism.","image":"","options":["Reabsorption from PCT","Reabsorption from Collecting Duct","Reabsorption/ Secretion in DCT","Counter current mechanism in Henle's loop/ Vasa recta"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Key fact: fluid flows in opposite directions in the two limbs of Henle's loop and in the two limbs of the vasa recta. This counter-current flow, helped by NaCl and urea, builds an increasing osmolarity in the medullary interstitium, from about 300 to 1200 mOsm/L.\nThis gradient draws water out of the collecting duct, which lets the kidney produce concentrated urine.\nReabsorption from the collecting duct is the tempting choice. But that reabsorption depends on the gradient that the counter-current mechanism creates.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Exemplar Biology Class 11, Ch 19 (MCQ Q14)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 16 · Excretory Products and their Elimination","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-EXCRETORY-164","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Excretory Products and their Elimination","qno":164,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Excretory Products and their Elimination","type":"mcq","question":"A fall in glomerular blood pressure activates certain cells of the kidney to release renin. These cells are located in the","image":"","options":["juxta glomerular apparatus","collecting duct","podocytes of Bowman's capsule","descending limb of Henle's loop"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The JGA forms where the distal convoluted tubule touches the afferent arteriole. Cells in both are modified at this point.\nA fall in glomerular blood flow, glomerular blood pressure or GFR activates the JG cells, and they release renin.\nPodocytes only form the filtration slits of Bowman's capsule. They do not secrete renin.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 16 · Excretory Products and their Elimination","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-EXCRETORY-165","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Excretory Products and their Elimination","qno":165,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Excretory Products and their Elimination","type":"mcq","question":"A diagram of a longitudinal section of the human kidney shows collecting ducts running through a medullary pyramid towards the hilum. Which sequence correctly traces the path of urine from the collecting duct to the outside of the body?","image":"","options":["Collecting duct → renal pelvis → calyx → ureter → urinary bladder → urethra","Collecting duct → calyx → renal pelvis → urethra → urinary bladder → ureter","Collecting duct → column of Bertini → renal pelvis → ureter → urinary bladder → urethra","Collecting duct → calyx → renal pelvis → ureter → urinary bladder → urethra"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Collecting ducts open into the renal pelvis through the medullary pyramids in the calyces, so a calyx comes before the pelvis.\nThe renal pelvis leads into the ureter, which carries urine to the urinary bladder.\nUrine is released from the bladder through the urethra.\nOption (1) reverses the calyx and the pelvis.\nOption (3) wrongly routes urine through a column of Bertini, which is cortical tissue.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 16 · Excretory Products and their Elimination","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-EXCRETORY-166","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Excretory Products and their Elimination","qno":166,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Excretory Products and their Elimination","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about the counter current mechanism in the human kidney are correct?\n(i) NaCl transported out of the ascending limb of Henle's loop is exchanged with the descending limb of vasa recta.\n(ii) The ascending portion of vasa recta returns NaCl to the interstitium.\n(iii) Small amounts of urea enter the thin segment of the ascending limb and are carried back to the interstitium by the collecting tubule.\n(iv) Interstitial osmolarity falls from about 1200 mOsmol L$^{-1}$ in the cortex to about 300 mOsmol L$^{-1}$ in the inner medulla.\n(v) The filtrate becomes progressively more concentrated as it moves up the ascending limb.","image":"","options":["Two","Three","Four","Five"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: NaCl from the ascending limb of Henle's loop is exchanged with the descending vasa recta. \n(ii) Correct: the ascending vasa recta returns NaCl to the interstitium. \n(iii) Correct: urea enters the thin ascending limb and is returned to the interstitium by the collecting tubule. \n(iv) Incorrect: the gradient rises from 300 mOsmol L$^{-1}$ in the cortex to 1200 mOsmol L$^{-1}$ in the inner medulla. \n(v) Incorrect: the filtrate becomes diluted in the ascending limb because electrolytes leave and water cannot follow. \nSo three statements are correct. \nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 16 · Excretory Products and their Elimination","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-EXCRETORY-167","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Excretory Products and their Elimination","qno":167,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Excretory Products and their Elimination","type":"mcq","question":"In kidney transplantation, why is the donor preferably a close relative of the patient?","image":"","options":["To minimise the chances of rejection by the recipient's immune system","Because a relative's kidney has more nephrons","To avoid the need for an anticoagulant during surgery","Because a relative's kidney produces more renin"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Kidney transplantation is the ultimate method of correcting acute renal failure.\nNCERT notes that a kidney from a close relative is preferred because it minimises the chance of rejection by the host's immune system.\nThe other options give reasons that have nothing to do with donor selection, such as nephron number, anticoagulants or renin output.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 16 · Excretory Products and their Elimination","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-EXCRETORY-168","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Excretory Products and their Elimination","qno":168,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Excretory Products and their Elimination","type":"mcq","question":"Dialysing unit (artificial kidney) contains a fluid which is almost same as plasma except that it has","image":"","options":["High glucose","High urea","No urea","High uric acid"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Key fact: in haemodialysis, the dialysing fluid has the same composition as plasma except that it lacks nitrogenous wastes such as urea.\nBecause the fluid contains no urea, urea diffuses out of the patient's blood into the fluid along its concentration gradient. The purified blood is then returned to the body.\nHigh urea or high uric acid in the fluid would stop these wastes from leaving the blood.\nHigh glucose in the fluid would disturb the patient's normal blood composition.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Exemplar Biology Class 11, Ch 19 (MCQ Q15)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 16 · Excretory Products and their Elimination","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-EXCRETORY-169","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Excretory Products and their Elimination","qno":169,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Excretory Products and their Elimination","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II:\nList-I\n(a) Proximal convoluted tubule\n(b) Descending limb of Henle's loop\n(c) Ascending limb of Henle's loop\n(d) Collecting duct\nList-II\n(i) Impermeable to water; electrolytes pass out\n(ii) Lets small amounts of urea into the medullary interstitium\n(iii) Brush border epithelium; reabsorbs 70–80% of electrolytes and water\n(iv) Permeable to water but almost impermeable to electrolytes","image":"","options":["a-iii, b-iv, c-i, d-ii","a-iii, b-i, c-iv, d-ii","a-ii, b-iv, c-i, d-iii","a-iv, b-iii, c-ii, d-i"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(a)–(iii): the PCT has simple cuboidal brush border epithelium and reabsorbs 70–80% of electrolytes and water. \n(b)–(iv): the descending limb is permeable to water but almost impermeable to electrolytes, so the filtrate becomes concentrated. \n(c)–(i): the ascending limb is impermeable to water, and electrolytes leave it, so the filtrate becomes dilute. \n(d)–(ii): the collecting duct lets a little urea into the interstitium to maintain osmolarity. \nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 16 · Excretory Products and their Elimination","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-EXCRETORY-170","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Excretory Products and their Elimination","qno":170,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Excretory Products and their Elimination","type":"mcq","question":"A diagram of the renal corpuscle shows the inner layer of Bowman's capsule closely wrapped around the glomerular capillaries. This layer is made of cells arranged in an intricate manner, leaving minute gaps between them. Identify these cells and the gaps.","image":"","options":["Podocytes; filtration slits","JG cells; slit pores","Brush border cuboidal cells; microvilli","Endothelial cells; basement membrane"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The epithelial cells of Bowman's capsule are called podocytes.\nThey are arranged so that minute spaces called filtration slits (slit pores) are left between them.\nJG cells belong to the afferent arteriole and DCT region, not the capsule.\nBrush border epithelium lines the PCT.\nEndothelium belongs to the capillary wall, not the capsule.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 16 · Excretory Products and their Elimination","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-EXCRETORY-171","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Excretory Products and their Elimination","qno":171,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Excretory Products and their Elimination","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about haemodialysis is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["Heparin is added to the blood before it enters the dialysing unit","The dialysing fluid has the same composition as plasma except for nitrogenous wastes","Cleared blood is returned to the body through an artery after adding heparin","Nitrogenous wastes move out of the blood across the porous cellophane membrane along a concentration gradient"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: the anticoagulant heparin is added to blood drained from an artery so that it does not clot in the unit. \n(2) Correct: the dialysing fluid matches plasma but contains no nitrogenous wastes. \n(3) Incorrect: cleared blood returns through a vein, and anti-heparin (not heparin) is added before it goes back. \n(4) Correct: wastes diffuse out across the cellophane membrane because the dialysing fluid lacks them. \nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 16 · Excretory Products and their Elimination","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-EXCRETORY-172","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Excretory Products and their Elimination","qno":172,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Excretory Products and their Elimination","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: In bony fishes, ammonia is mostly excreted as ammonium ions by diffusion across the gill surfaces.\nStatement II: In ammonotelic animals, the kidneys play the major role in removing ammonia.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. Ammonia is readily soluble, so it leaves the body by diffusion across body surfaces. In fish it leaves through the gill surfaces as ammonium ions.\nStatement II is incorrect. NCERT states that the kidneys do not play any significant role in removing ammonia.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 16 · Excretory Products and their Elimination","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-EXCRETORY-173","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Excretory Products and their Elimination","qno":173,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Excretory Products and their Elimination","type":"mcq","question":"In ammonotelic bony fishes, ammonia is mainly eliminated","image":"","options":["as uric acid crystals through the kidneys","as urea after conversion in the liver","as ammonium ions by diffusion across gill surfaces","as ammonia gas through the swim bladder"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Ammonia is readily soluble in water.\nIt is generally excreted by diffusion across body surfaces, or across gill surfaces in fish, in the form of ammonium ions.\nKidneys play no significant role in removing ammonia, so option (1) is wrong.\nConversion to urea in the liver happens in ureotelic animals, not in ammonotelic ones, so option (2) is wrong.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 16 · Excretory Products and their Elimination","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-EXCRETORY-174","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Excretory Products and their Elimination","qno":174,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Excretory Products and their Elimination","type":"mcq","question":"The proximal convoluted tubule (PCT) of the human nephron is lined by which type of epithelium?","image":"","options":["Simple squamous epithelium","Simple cuboidal brush border epithelium","Stratified squamous epithelium","Pseudostratified ciliated columnar epithelium"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT states that the PCT is lined by simple cuboidal brush border epithelium.\nThe brush border (microvilli) greatly increases the surface area for reabsorption. This suits the PCT, which reabsorbs nearly all essential nutrients and 70-80% of electrolytes and water.\nSimple squamous epithelium is thin and flat. It suits diffusion, not bulk reabsorption, so option (1) is the tempting but wrong choice.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 16 · Excretory Products and their Elimination","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-EXCRETORY-175","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Excretory Products and their Elimination","qno":175,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Excretory Products and their Elimination","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II:\nList-I\n(A) Ammonotelism\n(B) Ureotelism\n(C) Uricotelism\n(D) Route of ammonia removal in fish\nList-II\n(i) Land snails\n(ii) Marine fishes\n(iii) Aquatic insects\n(iv) Gill surfaces\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["A-(iii), B-(ii), C-(i), D-(iv)","A-(ii), B-(iii), C-(i), D-(iv)","A-(iii), B-(i), C-(ii), D-(iv)","A-(iv), B-(ii), C-(i), D-(iii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) Ammonotelism matches (iii): aquatic insects excrete ammonia.\n(B) Ureotelism matches (ii): marine fishes mainly excrete urea.\n(C) Uricotelism matches (i): land snails excrete uric acid.\n(D) Fish lose ammonia as ammonium ions across the gill surfaces, so D matches (iv).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 16 · Excretory Products and their Elimination","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-EXCRETORY-176","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Excretory Products and their Elimination","qno":176,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Excretory Products and their Elimination","type":"mcq","question":"The condition of accumulation of urea in the blood is termed as","image":"","options":["Renal Calculi","Glomerulonephritis","Uremia","Ketonuria"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Key fact: when the kidneys malfunction, urea builds up in the blood. This condition is called uremia, and it can lead to kidney failure.\nUremia is managed by haemodialysis, which removes urea from the blood.\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- Renal calculi are kidney stones.\n- Glomerulonephritis is inflammation of the glomeruli.\n- Ketonuria is the presence of ketone bodies in the urine.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Exemplar Biology Class 11, Ch 19 (MCQ Q10)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 16 · Excretory Products and their Elimination","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-EXCRETORY-177","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Excretory Products and their Elimination","qno":177,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Excretory Products and their Elimination","type":"mcq","question":"A drug selectively blocks the transport of NaCl out of the ascending limb of Henle's loop. Which outcome is most likely?","image":"","options":["Medullary interstitial osmolarity rises above 1200 mOsmol$L^{-1}$, and a small volume of highly concentrated urine is produced","Glomerular filtration stops completely, and no urine is formed","The medullary osmolarity gradient falls, less water is reabsorbed from the collecting duct, and a larger volume of less concentrated urine is produced","Urea recycling stops, but urine concentration stays unchanged because NaCl does not contribute to the gradient"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Step 1: NaCl pumped out by the ascending limb is a major contributor to the medullary gradient.\nStep 2: If this transport is blocked, interstitial osmolarity falls.\nStep 3: The gradient is what draws water out of the collecting tubule. With a weaker gradient, less water leaves.\nStep 4: The result is a larger volume of more dilute urine.\nOption 4 is wrong because NaCl is one of the two main solutes of the gradient.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 16 · Excretory Products and their Elimination","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-EXCRETORY-178","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Excretory Products and their Elimination","qno":178,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Excretory Products and their Elimination","type":"mcq","question":"Different types of excretory structures and animals are given below. Match them appropriately and mark the correct answer from among those given below:\nExcretory structure/organ — Animals\nA. Protonephridia — i. Prawn\nB. Nephridia — ii. Cockroach\nC. Malpighian tubules — iii. Earthworm\nD. Green gland or Antennal gland — iv. Flatworms","image":"","options":["A-iv, B-iii, C-ii, D-i","A-iii, B-i, C-iv, D-ii","A-iii, B-iv, C-ii, D-i","A-iii, B-i, C-ii, D-iv"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A. Protonephridia (flame cells) are found in flatworms such as Planaria: A–iv.\nB. Nephridia are the excretory organs of annelids such as the earthworm: B–iii.\nC. Malpighian tubules are found in insects such as the cockroach: C–ii.\nD. Green glands, also called antennal glands, are found in crustaceans such as the prawn: D–i.\nThis combination matches option (1).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Exemplar Biology Class 11, Ch 19 (MCQ Q6)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 16 · Excretory Products and their Elimination","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-EXCRETORY-179","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Excretory Products and their Elimination","qno":179,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Excretory Products and their Elimination","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about haemodialysis are correct?\n(i) Blood is drained from a convenient artery.\n(ii) An anticoagulant such as heparin is added before the blood enters the dialysing unit.\n(iii) The dialysing fluid has the same composition as plasma except for nitrogenous wastes.\n(iv) The cleared blood is returned to the body through an artery after adding anti-heparin.\n(v) Substances cross the porous cellophane membrane according to their concentration gradient.","image":"","options":["2","3","5","4"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"(i) Correct. Blood is taken from a convenient artery.\n(ii) Correct. Heparin is added before the blood is pumped into the unit, to prevent clotting.\n(iii) Correct. The dialysing fluid lacks nitrogenous wastes, so these wastes diffuse out of the blood into it.\n(iv) Incorrect. The cleared blood is returned through a vein, not an artery.\n(v) Correct. Movement across the cellophane membrane follows the concentration gradient.\nSo 4 statements are correct.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 16 · Excretory Products and their Elimination","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-EXCRETORY-180","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Excretory Products and their Elimination","qno":180,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Excretory Products and their Elimination","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following are secreted into the filtrate by the distal convoluted tubule?\n$H^+$, $K^+$, $NH_3$, $HCO_3^-$, glucose","image":"","options":["Two","Four","Five","Three"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"$H^+$ is secreted by the DCT, so it counts.\n$K^+$ is secreted by the DCT, so it counts.\n$NH_3$ is secreted by the DCT, so it counts.\n$HCO_3^-$ does not count: the DCT reabsorbs it rather than secreting it.\nGlucose does not count: it is reabsorbed, almost entirely in the PCT.\nThree substances are secreted.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 16 · Excretory Products and their Elimination","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-EXCRETORY-181","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Excretory Products and their Elimination","qno":181,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Excretory Products and their Elimination","type":"mcq","question":"In a human nephron, the glomerulus is a tuft of capillaries formed by which vessel?","image":"","options":["Efferent arteriole, a branch of the renal vein","Afferent arteriole, a fine branch of the renal artery","Vasa recta, a branch of the peritubular capillaries","Afferent arteriole, a fine branch of the renal vein"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT describes the glomerulus as a tuft of capillaries formed by the afferent arteriole. The afferent arteriole is a fine branch of the renal artery.\nThe efferent arteriole carries blood away from the glomerulus, so it does not form it.\nThe renal vein carries blood out of the kidney, so it cannot give rise to the afferent arteriole.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 16 · Excretory Products and their Elimination","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-EXCRETORY-182","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Excretory Products and their Elimination","qno":182,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Excretory Products and their Elimination","type":"mcq","question":"During haemodialysis, which substance is added to the blood before it is pumped into the dialysing unit?","image":"","options":["Anti-heparin","Renin","Heparin","Angiotensin II"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Blood taken from a convenient artery would clot in the machine. An anticoagulant, heparin, is therefore added before the blood enters the artificial kidney.\nAnti-heparin is the tempting wrong option. It is added only at the end, before the cleared blood is returned through a vein.\nRenin and angiotensin II take part in GFR regulation and have no role in dialysis.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 16 · Excretory Products and their Elimination","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-EXCRETORY-183","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Excretory Products and their Elimination","qno":183,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Excretory Products and their Elimination","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following combinations of animal, excretory structure and main nitrogenous waste is correct?","image":"","options":["Earthworm – Malpighian tubules – urea","Cockroach – Malpighian tubules – uric acid","Prawn – flame cells – uric acid","Planaria – green glands – ammonia"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The cockroach is a terrestrial insect. It excretes through Malpighian tubules and, like other insects, is uricotelic, so option (2) is correct.\nOption (1) fails because the earthworm uses nephridia, not Malpighian tubules.\nOption (3) fails because the prawn uses green (antennal) glands, not flame cells.\nOption (4) fails because Planaria uses flame cells, not green glands.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 16 · Excretory Products and their Elimination","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-EXCRETORY-184","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Excretory Products and their Elimination","qno":184,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Excretory Products and their Elimination","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about nephron organisation is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["The DCTs of many nephrons open into a common collecting duct","Collecting ducts converge and open into the renal pelvis through the medullary pyramids in the calyces","Most nephrons in the human kidney are juxtamedullary, with long loops of Henle reaching deep into the medulla","Peritubular capillaries arise from the efferent arteriole emerging from the glomerulus"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: many DCTs open into one straight collecting duct.\n(2) Correct: collecting ducts converge and open into the renal pelvis through the medullary pyramids in the calyces.\n(3) Incorrect: the majority of nephrons are cortical, with short loops. Only some nephrons are juxtamedullary.\n(4) Correct: the efferent arteriole forms the peritubular capillary network.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 16 · Excretory Products and their Elimination","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-EXCRETORY-185","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Excretory Products and their Elimination","qno":185,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Excretory Products and their Elimination","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Apart from promoting water reabsorption, ADH can also increase the glomerular filtration rate.\nReason (R): ADH constricts blood vessels, which raises blood pressure and thereby increases glomerular blood flow.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: according to NCERT, ADH also affects kidney function through its effect on blood vessels and can raise GFR.\nR is true: ADH is a vasoconstrictor, so it raises blood pressure.\nHigher blood pressure increases glomerular blood flow, and this in turn raises GFR.\nR therefore correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 16 · Excretory Products and their Elimination","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-EXCRETORY-186","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Excretory Products and their Elimination","qno":186,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Excretory Products and their Elimination","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: NaCl transported out by the ascending limb of Henle's loop is exchanged with the descending limb of vasa recta, and the ascending portion of vasa recta returns NaCl to the interstitium.\nStatement II: Small amounts of urea enter the descending limb of Henle's loop and are carried back to the interstitium by the proximal convoluted tubule.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. The ascending limb of Henle's loop moves NaCl out, and the descending vasa recta picks it up. The ascending vasa recta then returns it to the interstitium.\nStatement II is incorrect. Urea enters the thin segment of the ascending limb, not the descending limb. It is carried back to the interstitium by the collecting tubule, not by the PCT.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 16 · Excretory Products and their Elimination","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-EXCRETORY-187","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Excretory Products and their Elimination","qno":187,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Excretory Products and their Elimination","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following are situated in the cortical region of the human kidney?\nMalpighian corpuscle, proximal convoluted tubule, distal convoluted tubule, loop of Henle of a juxtamedullary nephron, medullary pyramids","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Malpighian corpuscle: in the cortex, as stated by NCERT.\nPCT: in the cortex.\nDCT: in the cortex.\nLoop of Henle of a juxtamedullary nephron: not in the cortex, because it runs deep into the medulla.\nMedullary pyramids: not in the cortex, because they are conical masses of the medulla projecting into the calyces.\nSo 3 of the listed structures are cortical.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 16 · Excretory Products and their Elimination","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-EXCRETORY-188","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Excretory Products and their Elimination","qno":188,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Excretory Products and their Elimination","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Tubular secretion is an important step in urine formation.\nReason (R): Tubular cells secrete H$^+$, K$^+$ and ammonia into the filtrate, which helps maintain the ionic and acid-base balance of body fluids.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. NCERT describes tubular secretion as an important step in urine formation.\nR is true. The tubules secrete H$^+$, K$^+$ and ammonia, and this maintains ionic and acid-base balance.\nR gives the reason why secretion is important, so R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 16 · Excretory Products and their Elimination","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-EXCRETORY-189","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Excretory Products and their Elimination","qno":189,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Excretory Products and their Elimination","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (organ/gland) with List-II (excretory product eliminated):\nList-I\nA. Sebaceous glands\nB. Liver\nC. Sweat glands\nD. Lungs\nList-II\n(i) About 200 mL of $CO_2$ per minute\n(ii) Bilirubin and biliverdin\n(iii) Sterols, hydrocarbons and waxes\n(iv) NaCl and lactic acid\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["A-iv, B-ii, C-iii, D-i","A-iii, B-i, C-iv, D-ii","A-iii, B-ii, C-iv, D-i","A-ii, B-iii, C-i, D-iv"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A-iii: Sebum from sebaceous glands carries sterols, hydrocarbons and waxes.\nB-ii: Bile secreted by the liver contains bilirubin and biliverdin.\nC-iv: Sweat is a watery fluid containing NaCl, small amounts of urea, and lactic acid.\nD-i: The lungs remove about 200 mL of $CO_2$ per minute, along with water.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 16 · Excretory Products and their Elimination","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-EXCRETORY-190","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Excretory Products and their Elimination","qno":190,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Excretory Products and their Elimination","type":"mcq","question":"A healthy person has a GFR of 125 ml/minute and passes 1.5 litres of urine per day. What percentage of the daily filtrate is reabsorbed by the renal tubules? (Take 1 day = 1440 minutes.)","image":"","options":["98.33%","99.17%","99.92%","83.33%"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: GFR = 125 ml/min and urine = 1.5 L/day.\nFiltrate per day = 125 × 1440 = 180000 ml = 180 L.\nVolume reabsorbed = 180 − 1.5 = 178.5 L.\nPercentage reabsorbed = (178.5 / 180) × 100 ≈ 99.17%.\nThis matches NCERT's figure of nearly 99 per cent.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 16 · Excretory Products and their Elimination","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-EXCRETORY-191","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Excretory Products and their Elimination","qno":191,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Excretory Products and their Elimination","type":"mcq","question":"The term 'counter current' in the mechanism of urine concentration refers to:","image":"","options":["Flow of filtrate and blood in the same direction in the nephron and peritubular capillaries","Flow of filtrate in opposite directions in the two limbs of Henle's loop, and of blood in opposite directions in the two limbs of vasa recta","Movement of water and NaCl in the same direction across the descending limb","Flow of urine from the collecting duct back into the distal convoluted tubule"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The two limbs of Henle's loop carry filtrate in opposite directions, which forms a counter current.\nThe two limbs of the U-shaped vasa recta also carry blood in opposite directions.\nThis opposite flow, together with the close placement of the loop and the vasa recta, maintains the gradient.\nFlow in the same direction (option 1) would be a concurrent flow, not a counter current.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 16 · Excretory Products and their Elimination","difficulty":"easy"}]});
